        <p>From Dr. B. J. BoIILTON AND Co., Horncastle. We have made pretty extensive use of Chlorodyne In our practice lately and look upon it as an excellent direct Seda- tive and Anti-spasmodic. It seems to allay pain and irrita- tion In whatever organ, and from whetever cause. It inddlies a feeling cf comfort and quietude rot obtainable byany other remedy, and it seems to posses this great advantage over all other sedatives ; that It leaves no unpleasant after effects. •• Rarl Russell communicated to the College of Physicians that he received a despatch from her Majesty's Codgul at Manilla to the effect that Cholera had been raging fee:V.- and that the ONLY remedy of any service was CHLO - DINR."—See Lancet, lit December. 1864. CAUTION.—BRWARE OF PIRACY AND IMITA- TIONB. CAITTION.—Vice-Chanoellor Sir W. PAGE WOOD stated that Dr. J. COLLIS BRoWNE was. undoubtedly, the inventor cf "CHLORODYNE " and that the story of the defendant FREEMAN, was deliberately untrue, which be regretted to say had been sworn to.—See Zimes, 13th July, 1864. Sold in bottles at Is. 14.4„, Es. 9d., and 4s. 6d. Nona is genuine without the words "DR. J. COLLIS BROWNE'S CHLORODYBR "on the Government Stamp. Overwhelm, lug Mediosl testimony accompanies each bottle</p>
