      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0004" n="art0004">
        <head>Cintlina McDOUGALL'S PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR</head>
        <p>Cintlina McDOUGALL'S PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>• , e, ' .1, ,</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>THE best cure for a love is another one. MANY a sweetheart has a sour disposition. EVERY man thinks he is the best friend some other man ever had. WOMEN talk a lot about model husbands but everyone wants a different model. A GENIUS is a man who never makes the same mistake twic,.. THE approv-il of your conscience is an excellent thing, but it is not sulicient to get you a rise of salary.</p>
        <p>ALL the world may love a lover, but if he attempts to reciprocate, his sweetheart is likely to object. SHE: "I have been elected treasurer of the club." He: "Goodness l I suppose I'll be con- tinually drawing cheques to balance the cash." THE sensible man never complains. If he breaks his leg he is always thankful that it isn't his neck.</p>
        <p>HE KNEW JONES.—Smith: "See Jones over there laughing so heartily. Somebody must have told him a funny story." Brown: "More likely Jones told it himself.' NowEERE NEAE IT. —Morgan : "Do you be- lieve that a woman will deliberately lie about her ago?" Jorgan : "About it? Oh, dear, no; no- where near it." MOTHER (reprovingly): "You shouldn't make light of marriage, my daughter." Daughter (in- dignantly): "I think it's all right to make light of a match." BOARD= (savagely) : "This fr,od isn't fit for a pig to eat, madam." Boardieg-House Keeper (sarcastically): "Then no wonder you are dis- satisfied with it." MAMMA: "And how did my little pet get to sleep last night without mamma ? " Little Pet: "Papa died to sing to Inc like you do, and I hurried up an' went to sleep so's not to hear it." "I THINK I know now, said the soldier who was making a determined effort to masticate his first ration of army beef, "what people mean when they talk about the sinews of war." "HAVE you finished ploughing the ten-acre field ?" asked Farmer Richland of his son. " Then you may now attend to the harrowing details."</p>
        <p>THEY WILL TALK.—" And did they all live to tell the tale ?" asked the listener. "Yes," replied the narrator; "and they have been doing nothing else ever since." YOUNG- Lain (examining bridal veils) : "Can you really recommend this one ? " Over-Zealous Shopman "Oh, yes, miss llt may be used several times." ComruNsArouv.—Mrs. Brown: "We missed you in the conversation so much, my dear." Mrs. Jones: "I'm so sorry." Mrs. Brown: "But than, of course, your absence made a lot of talk." WITH MEANING.—Mrs. Jones, "Just think, Mrs. Brown makes all her own dresses." Jones the Heartless: "I always wondered what made them look and fit so welL" ACCOUNTED Fou.—Traveller : "Who was that man whom I overheard denouncing the new town hall as a death trap?" Villaga Merchant : "That was the architect who didn't plan it." " GWENDOLINE," said the mother to the giddy forty-year-old daughter, ":f a young man were to kiss you, what would you do?" "I should kiss him back," replied the frivolous young maiden. "And serve him jolly well right, too 1" exclaimed the father in ths background. THE BEST Ono.—Little Willy: "Pa, is the best man at a wedding the one that gets married ?" Mr. Hennypecip: "No, my son; the best man is one who does not get married. BY COMPARISON.—" Define hope." "Well, it is something like daylight; we generally have a lot of it when we get up, and by bedtime we haven't any." SILLY FELLOW.—" He is the silliest boy you ever heard of. He writes me twice a day." "How absolutely foolish, Nell! What does he do the rest of the day?" "He says he spends it in reading the letters I write him. Nov HIS FAULT.—Doctor: "That's a bad razor cut in your head, Rastus. Why don't you profit by this lesson and keep out: of bad company?" Rastus : "Ah would, doctah, but Ah ain't got no money toe git er divorce." KEEPING IN S'TYLE.—Brieket : "What can I send you to-day, Mrs. Styles ? " Mrs. S.: "Send me a leg of mutton, and be sure it is from a black sheep." Brisket: "A black sheep?" Mrs. 5.: " Yes ; we are in mourning, you know." SHOP TALK.—" Papa's mind is full of business all the time." "How does that trouble you?" "Well, when Harry asked him for me, he said, 'Yes, take her along, and if she isn't up to our advertisement, bring her back and exchange her."</p>
        <p>Alms. I'ECR : "Henry, when I married you" Mr. Peck: "There, Pm glad you've made •up your mind to admit it at last." A momen later the neighbours saw him running down the street without his hat. AN ERROR IN TAcrics.—What is the use of talking ? " asked the married man, incautiously. As his wife indulged in twenty minutes' animated discussion of this question and then resumed the thread of her remarks he perceived that he had made a mistake.; His IDEA OP IT. —"Now, that is what I call criminal carelessness," said Swipesy, the burglar, as he read the paper the morning after the robbery. "What is criminal carelessness 1" asked his wife. "The paper said that right by the safe I cracked there was a table, and in the drawer of the table there was seventeen big diamonds and five hun- dred dollars in gold coin. It was criminally care- less in me to overlook all that swag." His FEELiso.—Mrs. Hoon : "I think, dear, that this apparent misfortune is really a blessing in dieguise."*Hoon " Huh ! That may be; but it will have to establish its identity before I accept</p>
        <p>It with resignation." AN ULTIMATGM. —" There's no use talking," re- marked Bobby, glumly; "things have got to he changed so's I can do something once in a while that I really want to do without being wicked, or I'm a goner, that's all I" " JOEIN HENRY, it isn't any good, I know, that keeps you down town so late, when you ought to be at home with me and the children." (Shrug. ging his shoulders) " Honi soit qui mal y pens?, my dear." "That's right! Swear at your wife in Latin." ONE CONSOLATION. —Emily : "I am so unhappy. I begin to see that Arthur married me for my money." Her dearest friend: "Well, you have the comfort of knowing that he is not so simple as he looks." As Ha SAID. —Uncle : "I don't know about lending you any more money. When I lent you money two months ago you said you wanted it only for a little while." Nephew: "And I didn't have it more than half an hour." PRESENCE OF MIND. —Getthar : "I called up the Van Kickers by 'phone last evening to find out if Van Kicker was there before calling on the daughter, and the old man answered it himself." Goodun : "The deuce! What did you do 1" Getthar : "Told him his office building in town was on fire.' HER WAY.—They were spooning in a lane with many windings. "A woman will have her way, you know," said the fair girl, archly. " Yes ; even if she has to knock a man down to get it," ho retorted, with illustrative presence of mind, as the inevit- able feminine scorcher swept round he corner and laid him prostrate. SHE "So this is your picture? It is a true re. presentation of the dining.room on the ocean steamer; but why didn't you introduce acme characters?" He: "Because that picture Is entitled The Dinner Hour during a Rough Passage.'" VERY TRYING.—" This weather is very trying for everybody," said the family physician. "Yes," replied Mr. Meekton. "I don't see how my wife is going to bear up under it. When the sun doein't shine it gives her the 13: ac, ELIA when it does she says it's fading the carpet. Hu WAS a great bore, a 1,1 was talking to a crowd about the coming lona eiecti ,a. Said he :—"Gibbs is a good man; he is capable, honest, fearless, and conscientious. Ha will make the very kind of re- presentative we need. He once saved my life from drowning." "Do you really want to see Gibbs elected?" said a solemn-faced man. "I do, indeed. I'd give anything to see him elected," answered the Lore. "Then never let anybody know he saved 'oer life," counselled the solemn-faced man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>MOST NUTRITIOUG.</head>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUG.</p>
        <p>P PS'S</p>
        <p>gRATEFUL-COMFORTING. r • -</p>
        <p>ZREAKFAST-eu PPER.</p>
        <p>SADIES' LE'rilEß:</p>
        <p>MY Dx,ta Nm.IJE,</p>
        <p>There are signs that:the fur toques continue tei be much in favour. Some of the newest shapes ere turned. up in front in what is known as the Empire or Napoleon style, while others have rather curious fur strings—in reality two narrow boas starting from the buck of the hat and fastening under the chin with a head and four or six little tails. All these toques, whether of sable, chin- chilla, or ermine, are trimmed with knots of flowers—violets- in most cases—or folds of tulle or gauze, affecidedly incongruous combination, un- doubtedly, but one on which Dame Fashion has set her seal of approval. Toques of pink, white, or turquoise-blue cloth tucked or corded, are also in vogue, rand a few- feather toques have made their appearance. Ostrich feather muffs, by the way, must be reckoned among the new year novelties, and are very pretty, although their beauty is sadly short-lived.</p>
        <p>I saw a black serge dress at a fashionable wen- ding with a black silk bolero much stitched, and scalloped out all round in squares, each of which was bordered with gold and bright green braid. A similar trimming outlined each seam, and the vest was white silk with closely-set rows of bright pale green and gold braid. The collar and under- sleeves matched the vest. On this gown the three-tier sleeve may be seen. The upper part is in the black serge; the medium portion in full black silk, and the mutton-like undersleeve is as described.</p>
        <p>The most becoming sleeve is one that follows the outline of the arm from shoulder to elbow, leaving the wrist comparatively free. The little Light undersleeve is.apt to give a wooden look to the wrist by robbing it of its curves, tr, rather, hiding them away. It is pretty safe to prophesy modification of the bell sleeve for the coming season's wear. Long sleeves in lace, chiffon,. or sequined net seem to be going quite out of fashion in favour of the elbow sleeve. This latter has cer- tainly enjoyed a very successful revival of late. Owners of pretty arms prefer the short sleeve, or that latest version of it, a'raost becoming one, which consists of a few folds of black chiffon loosely caught-round the upper arm.</p>
        <p>I think that one of the most striking novelties is the new blouse coat. The upper part of this is cut much on the same lines as the blouse in vogue a little time back, but a couple of inches above the waist-line it is set into a close-fitting corselet-belt of the same material, if the jacket is of cloth.; if Of fur, the corselet can be of cloth, ailk, or dull- surfaced satin according to taste. The coat is single-breasted, with a. rounded collar at the back that formsahort revesa in front. It is a.most.com- fortable shape, and eery_ becoming to-ordinary figuresvalthough should be eschewed by those who Lave-martendeecy to stoutness. The cellar, St-ehouldbesaid, is, as a, rule, of glac4mlktstitched, corded, or embroidered, if cloth 16 used. for "the coat; butin the-mot/fa fur jacket thercelhar-fln. variably matches the corselet. At present, the blouse-coat is oftenest seen in black cloth, or in sable or caracal, but in another month or two, when the days are longer and spring seems nearer at hand, it will probably appear in light colours, synth smart trimmings of braiding or possemen- Iterie.</p>
        <p>Dresses of almost any material, if properly packed, Will come out unerushed. If possible, have a dress-basket long enough to take the skirts with. out doubling, or, at any rate, so that they are only once doubled. Fold perfectly straight and smooth, putting tissue paper between tho folds of soft things, silks, satins, muslins, and the like, and plenty of tissue paper about frills and flounces, and in the sleeves of soft blouses. Lay overtly in the trunk, and press down firmly, leaving no room for the things to jostle each other. Serge or alpaca should never crease if properly packed. Soft. finish goods usually pack best, Oriental satin, AN.shraere, nun's veiling, and so on. Cheap " papery "satins and glaco silks will often crease, even when carefully packed, but when anything else comes out so creased that even ironing will not make it presentable, depend upon it the packer ia to blame. .</p>
        <p>Your loving JENNIL</p>
