      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Duke of Norfolk, whose recent visit to Rome has been the subject of so much comment, is not only Earl Marshal, but also Chief Butler of England. Triers are many privileges attaching to the office of Earl Marshal, and as Chief Butler the Dukes of Norfolk are entitled to the presentation of a gold cup on the occasion of a coron- atlas. 'There are of course many hereditary privileges held by various peers. Some of them are very curious, as, for instance, that of carver to the Sovereign, which belongs to the Earl of Denbigh. At the coronation of eorge IV., the Lord Denbigh of the day claimed the right, and had it conceded to him. Her Majesty's tastes at table are, and ever have been, peculiarly simple. Of soup the Sovereign Invariably partakes, white soups being especial favourites. Beef, boiled chicken, and a little game, form her staple food, the Queen caring little for elaborate dishes, or sweets of any kind. Of elJssert Her Majesty partakes freely, peaches and grapes from the Royal gardens, standing high he the Queen's favour. In addition to her almost priceless gold, and silver plate,the Queen possesses some magnificent china dinner services, which have been either purchased, inherited, or given to her by Royal Sovereigns and relations. A Minton dinner service is the one generally used on great ("elusions. The white china is trirdered with a lovely shade of blue, with dainty medallions of the reel, sham- , rock, and thistle, while in the centre i 3 a medallion bear- ing on it V.R. in raised letters of gold. A. green and gold Sevres service, given to the Queen by Napoleon 111. and one of Chantilly porcelain, are both much prized, while one old Chelsea service is so valuable that it is now never taken out of the china cupboard. Wedgwood figures are at the present time, in consider- able favour for table decoration, but for those who can not afford these very desirable items, the pretty lilac an, white china, now on the market makes an ex- cellent substitute: "On a South African ;Farm " is the title of a centre-piece, much in favour at the moment. This consists of a little nigger doll, an ostrich, and a Japanese umbrella. There is a rustic basket at the back of the nigger, and this is filled with flowers or ferns. Another novel table-centre is a Chinese tea house. The thin metal of which it is built becomes invisible when the framework is covered with flowers, and the centre-piece is placed on a sheet of looking-glass, surrounded with ferns, to suggest the lake In the willow-pattern plate. Jewellery for out-door wear, is in better taste this season than it has been for some time past. Mathes are worn on the bodice not on the coat, long chains with purses are partially tucked away, two curb bracelets, jewelled, or not, are considered quite enough, and noth- ing jangly is worn. Rowe of pearls too are not so often seen in the day-time as they were, and dog collars 'sever. Among distinguished possessors of floe pearls— apart from Royal folk—may be mentioned, the Duchess of Marlborough, who has a unique necklace of these gems. which once formed part of the French Crown jewels. Lady Wimborne also owns a necklace of excep- tionally fine pearls, while Lady Ilehester's necklace of black pearls is unique and was once the property of the Er:apron Eugenie. The turquoise it still in great request, despite all that has been raid lately to the contrary. Like most other gems, the turquoise looks best with white, but being an accommodating kind of stone it " swears" at very few colours. Perhaps worst of all, is the wrong blue, put with the stone, two keys of the same colour being more harrassing together than any diverse pairs of hues. turquoiee velvet of exactly the same shade as the stones, worn with a turquoise and diamond dog-collar, and turquoise and oiamond ornaments everywhere looks magnificent, but these rather traitorous stones are best when entrusted to pale yellow, or white for in damp weather their hue changes perceptibly. Brocade is cemparatively little worn. velvet and velveteen finding greater favour with dowagers and elderly chaperons nowadays. The colours of the newest velvets are beautiful beyond compare. There are the rich wine tints that range upwards through graduations of orange, mandarin, vermillion, yellow and citron, to warm cream and ivory tones. Then again there is the series of beautiful colours that begin with a bass note of pansy-purple, and ascend through a long scale of brightening hues, to the soft lilac of the westeria, while for evening wear there are white, and cream, and ivory, the pale flower tints in yellow, pink, scarlet, and green, and the brilliant turqueise blue, that is so effective in artificial light, Leading modistes invariably undertrim rather than overtrim the dekolletage, but one and all agree that pra bard velvet line against the skin is not becoming. Some softening element is therefore intro- duced—a flohu, baud of lace, scarf,or perchance a wisp of tulle.</p>
        <p>The heart-shaped aoolletage is decidedly the hest worn, and when well out and trimmed, is delightful, as ' it dieplaves the neck and shoulders in such a graceful form. For restaurant dinners and theatres this is filled in with some semi-transparent material, which detracts not at all from the fascinating whole. Flowers are in- dispensable aojuncts to;the success of an evening toil- ette. Parma violets are well to the fore, and on a gown of cream or antique lace, look particularly well. The right shoulder strap is frequently formed of them, from whence they trail across the edge of the docolletee and finish in a huge bunch at the left side. It is becoming quite the thing in town to adjourn to the more fashionable tea rooms for indulgenee in the afternoon cup. Everywhere the tendency of the day is to do one's entertaining at the pnblio establishments. Many dietinguished people in Society who never thought cf giving a dinner party except in their own house, now take a roam, or a table at &amp; fashionable restaurant. Society dinner parties are quite as common a feature at some of these places as supper parties, and one or two of the highest class establishments can frequently boast that their entire accommodation is taken by titled people. A similar chasse is taking place in the grow- ing practice of taking tea out. After the afternoon calls, it is quite a usual thing to resort to one of the Lea rocms, where the service is everything that could be detired, and the daintiest of cakes and other niceties are to be had. The making of coffee is unfortunately becoming a lost art, in this country. It Is so much easier, led in- deed cheaper, to put tea or cocoa on the breakfast table, that many people give up coffee altogether, and every year the Board of Trade returns show that we drink less and less of this article. This is partly accounted for by the difficulty in obtaining freshly roasted and freshly ground coffee. Then good coffee cannot be had for less than 2s per lb, or about the same as the price for a fairly good tea. But the latter gees twice as far as coffee, and there is a tendency with many housewives to try and make the coffee go as far as the tea, and con- sequently only poor results are obtained. To make a really good beverage, there should be fully one aid a half, or two ounces of the best ground coffee to one qusrt of liquid. &amp; filter bag should always be used in making coffee, and it is also Important that the hot milk ur cream should be put into the cup before pouring in the coffee. " MADGE."</p>
      </div>
