      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>_ SERIOUS CHARGES. A WARRINGTON POSTMAN COMMITTED Ta THE ASSIZES.</p>
        <p>Alleged Theft of Postal Orders.</p>
        <p>Serious charges were preferred against James Francle Gartland, a postman who resides at 77, Fox-street, at the Warrington Police Court on Monday. The magis- trates on the beech were Mr. F. Monks tin the chair), Alderman Heaton,Councillor Smetburst,Messrs. Birchall, J R Young, Thornton, Wright and Hayward. The prisoner was remanded a week age on the charge of stealing a letter containing a postal order for 25., and on Monday an additional charge of stealing a letter con- tianiug two 203. postal orders on the 30th of November, was brought against hinp—Mr. Paxtoa of Liverpool, conducted the prosecution on behalf of the Post Offize authorities, and the prisoner was represented by Mr. Arthur Browne. The first witness called was Edward Horrocks, a joiner, who lives at 69, Fox-street. He informed the Bench that on the 20M of November he made up a letter, enclosing in it a postal order for 2s. He made a note of the number of the order which was C. C. 63,700,385, and made it payable to S Reeves, of Liverpool. There was a letter in the evelope, addressed to Mr 9 Reeves bookseller, 446, Szoeland-road, Liverpool. He gave the letter to his son Edward to post. The order was pur- chased at the Sankey Green post office. i A 2s postal order was shown to the witness and he stated that the number corresponded with that of the one he sent away. The mimes of the payee and Paying office were not, however, filled in in his hand- writing. Edward Horrocks, junior, said he posted the letter in the letter-box in Lovely-lane. He purchased a postal order for his father at the Sankey Green post office on , the earn° day, but he did not remember what amount he 135 id for it. Evidence was then given by James Billinge, sub- , , Postmaster at the Smalley Green office. He stated that '. the 2e.postal order produced was issued by him on the 'e ' 20th ot November and bore his signature. He impressed a date stamp upon it at the time of issue. The order 1 s Foe? bore the date December 20;h, but this was not ' unpreeseil by his stamp. . In answer to Mr Browne witness said he could not remember to whom he sold the order. Alfred Buckley Mellor, inspector of postmen at War- tjelgton, said he knew the prisoner. On the 20th November last he was in the employ of the Post Office as an auxiliary postman. He was employed regularly at the Post Office till January sth, the morning of his arrest. It was the prisoner's duty to clear the Lovely- lane box at 8 .p.m, on the 20th November.—By Mr. Browne ! He could go further and sly he did clear the box. prisoner signed the attendance book at the office, showing that he performed this particular duty. Samuel Re,ves, newsagent, bookseller, Ste, Liverpool. . said he had not received a postal order ifor 2s from Mr Horrocks. He had, however, received a postcard . inquiring about the order. - Edward Alexandra Kirby, a clerk in the General : Post Office, London. spoke to interviewing the prisoner te at Warrington on the sth January, with regard to . numerous complaints which had recently been received ' 1 the loss of letters, containing postal orders and other e valtlakles passing through this office. He showed him a few postal orders and askei him if he had anything in - h'S posseseion belonging to the Poet Office, and he replied ' "No," Witness then asked him if he minded showieg him the contents of his pockets. At first he refused to , : do so, but subsevently he took from the pocket of the , Uniform coat he was wearing a letter which was in the unfastened envelope now produced. It contained three . Postal orders (which witness produced),and one of them i was the order reterred to as numbered C 63,700,385. -` They were the missing orders. He asked the prisoner if he could explain his position, and he replied "The two 2i; orders were purchased for me by a girl about 15 years 1 age who lives next door to me. Tue is 6d order I Purchased myself." Ile afterwards said he purchased the two Zs orders himself. lde told him the date stamp impressions on two of the orders had been tampered with, and that there were traces of erasure of writing on he 2; orders over which the name Pannett had been written. The reply was " I wrote the name Pannett on the oeders,but I have not interfered with the date stamp, nor do I know who has. The dates are just the same as when I purchased the orders." Witness then told him that the order for 23 was one of those which had been iequired for io papers be then had before him, and that the letter in which it Was stated to have been enclosed NW said to have been posted in the Lovely-lane Pillar box on the evening of the 20M November. He told him be would be charged with stealing the letter, and the reply Wati " I have not stolen it. Witness then gave the prisoner into the custody of P.C. Newey: By Mr Browne 1 Witness bad the prisoner's house "Itched, but nothing WaS found. I P.C. Newey said that when he took the prisoner into custody and charged him with the offence be said .. I Swear before God I have never taken anything." , Prisoner, who reserved his defence, was committed , for trial at the Liverpool assizeS. t . Evidence was then given with reference so the Second e charge. Frederick John Binks, beer retailer, 12. Leices- , . ter-street, Warrington, said that on the 30th November , ~ his father handed him two 20s. postal orders. The names ' of the payee and pay office on the orders produced were e . nos in his handwriting. He wrote a letter, put the ' orders into an envelope and addressed them to" Mies ' Mather, City Hospital, Edinburgh." Miss Mather had Since become his wife. He gave the envelope to his , father to post. Benjamin 13inke, grocer and beer retailer,Aiketi-street, said that on the 33th November last he purchased the . orders in question at the Warrincton Post Office. Be handed them to the last witness. Ile did not fill in the 1: name of the payee and pay office. The same evening ' his son gave him a letter eildressed to Miss Mather and ' he posted it in the pillar letter box in Priestley-street, . between eight and nine o'clock. . By Mr. Browne.—He believed it was nearer nine than i. eight. ~ Florence Binks, wife of Frederick John Sinks, 12, , Leieeeter-strest, said that on the Ist December last she e Was at the City Hospital, Edinburgh. She left there on ' the 11th December. She was then unmarried. She had received no postal orders from Mr Biuks. but she had . expected them. Alfred Buckley Mellor, again called, proved that the nrie ~ . Drier was on duty on tbe evening in question, from , 8.30 to 11.45. He was on station duty, and had to con- Vey the mails to the railway station. He bad also , duties to discharge in the office. There was a collection at the Priestley-.street box at 9.45. Mary Elizabeth Tonge, sub-postmistress at Sankey , Bridges Post Office, said that on the let December last che ca,bed the two postal orders for 20s each (along t with another one for ss). They were handed te her 1 , with the receipts signed 9 Smith and Sam Smith. She identified the prisoner as the person to whom she paid , the money on the orders. Be Mr Browne : Prisoner came to her office between 1 1 45 and twelve noon. She had reasons for paying d particular attention to him at the time. She made a [ niemorstiOnm at the time. Edward Alcx Kirby, again called, referred to his , interview with the prisoner on the sth January. He told him that the postal orders now producsd had been paid , at the Senkey B idees Post Office on the let December, and that his cleeeription corresponded with that of the Person to whom pet mmt was made ; also that the hendwriting of the names appearing as receipts to the , erders str neely resembled his. Prisoner's reply was, " I know nothing about the orders." He had compared a / peels:nen of primer's writing with that.sa the orders, ;,...</p>
