      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>MEETING OF THE STOCKTON HEATH PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>Opposition to the Light Railway Scheme.</p>
        <p>• - Mr. Mason presided at the meeting or the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee on Thursday last week. There were also present: Messrs Pearson, Clarke, Pember ton, Whitley, Fairhurst, and G F Ashton (clerk), .1 Farrington (nuisance inspector), and J Swinton (surveyor). BUILDING PLANS.-ALLEGED IRREGULARITY BY A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>BUILDER.</head>
        <p>BUILDER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swinton submitted the plans he had received for the erection of cottages in Dandonald Avenue for Mr Wright. —The plans wore approved on the proposition of Mr. Pearson, seconded by Mr. Pemberton. The Surveyor reported that Mr F W Wright had not built in accordance with the plans he had submitted for five houses in Dundonald-avenue. The plans showed a width of atroet of 3EI feet but he had built over the build- ing line and left the street of less width than that stipulated. A meeting of the Building Committee was held but the committee thought the alteration did not seriously interfere with the building line. Nevertheless, the committee did not approve of the action a Mr. Wright in not adhering to the plans. He (Mr. Swinton) noticed the irregularity at the time Mr. Wright had laid the foundations but in spite of him (the Surveyor) writ- ing to him on the matter Mr. Wright continued with the building. He (the Surveyor) informed Mr Wright that he was building on his own responsibility. The Chairman thought Mr Wright had treated the committee with scant courtesy. It was decided on the proposition of Mr Clarke that Mr Wright be requested to appear before the committee to explain his position in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>'WIDTH OF BACK PASSAGES.</head>
        <p>THE 'WIDTH OF BACK PASSAGES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton in accordance with notice of motion given moved that new bye-laws be adopted to regulate the width of streets which are used as secondary means of access to buildings. The Clerk informed the committee that the Frodshant Committee had adopted new bye-laws on this matter. The Local Government Bard had sanctioned 16 feet as the width of a passage over 300 feet long and 12 feet if 105 e than 300 feet long, the passage to be opea at both ends. Mr. Pemberton moved that the passages be the same width as in the Borough of Warrington, viz :—Ten feet wide up to 100 feat long ' • 15 feet up to 200 feet long ; and 20 feet over 300 feet long, the passages to be open at both ends. Mr Pearson said he preferred the Frodsham bye-laws. He did not agree with narrow back passages. Mr. Clarks suggested that the matter be deferred until the neat meeting, and this course was adopted.</p>
        <p>THE OBJECTIONS TO TUE LIGHT RAILWAY SCHEME, Mr Clarke proposed the following reselution :—" That this Committee respectfully requests the Runcorn Rural District Council to oppose the proposed Warrington and Northwich Light Railway for the following and other reasons :—That such a railway as this is proposed to be, would be a danger and annoyance to the residents of Stockton Heath ; (2) That the roads of the parish are not suitable nor are they made to be suitable for the purposes of such a railway ; (3) That in London-road, Stockton Heath, it ie proposed to take the railway past the entrance of the public schools where over 700 child- ren attend and if the railway is made the lives of these children will be in constant peril when going to or from the schools; (4) That we understand there is a contem- plated agreement between the promoters of the railway and the Warrington Corporation for the sale to tho latter of the proposed railway number 1 to the detriment of the ratepayers of this parish." 1u proposing this resolution, Mr. Clarke thought it was necessary for the Parochial Committee as a separate authority from the Parish Council to take the matter up. He need not say much about it, but he contended that such a railway would be a danger and annoyance to the residents of Stockton Heath. From what they had learned from Mr Scotter,the undertaking was nothing less than an ordinary railway. When Mr Scotter came to see the members of the Parish Council he told them it was the promoters intention to take any railway truck from the Cheshire lines at Northwich,and although they might not be able to put thorn on the light I railway lines they would carry the trucks on a bogey. Mr Scotter told them it was their intention to get hold of as much traffic as they possibly could, for if the railway was to be made to pay, half the revenue. must come from goods traffic. One terminus of the railway would be at Stockton Heath, and if they were going to be saddled with an ordinary railway like this with long luggage trairstr, then ai it would not only be a danger but an annoyance to atepayers. It was only right and proper that steps shouldr be taken to stop such a eroceeding. Dealiog with the second objection relating to roads, he said it was not the intention of the company to widen them at Stockton Heath except in one or two instances. The roads were not meant for a light railway ; they were the King's highway and did not belong to the County Council of Cheshire or the Rural District Council, but to the whole of the people in the kingdom. Everybody hie a perfect right to go over the road without let or hindrance and therefore the appropriation of part of the road by a railway was wrong and was putting I the road to a purpose which was not legitimate 1 The third objection respecting the danger to school children needed no amplification. With regard to the proposed agreement with the Warrington Corporation, he believed the promoters were proposing under certain Conditions to sell this lima e as soon as they get it, to the Warrington Corporation, apnudtiifn tahveeyrydiwd r orl no ,i 4 SptoOsCi kt itoOnn. Ine Theatwh arartrieupaa tycien"Cwo;pucl,;abtieon desired to bring their tram- ways into Stockton Heath, and the most straightfor- ward way would be to make terms with the authorities of Stockton Heath and not try to get the power in such 1 , an underhand way as that proposed. He (Mr Clarke) did not call it a light railway. Mr Scotter was unable to tell them the difference between the proposed railway , and an ordinary railway. Mr Pemberton : There is no difference. Mr. Clarke considered that the Committee was justi- fied in asking for the assistance of the district council to help them in opposing the scheme. It was not often that the District Council did anything for Stockton Heath, and this was an opportunity for the District Council to redeem itself. Mr. Pamberton was strongly of opinion that the Parish Emacil ought to have the right to purchase the</p>
        <p>line. The Clerk said he believed that hi the last order of the proineters there was a clause enabling the District Conn. cil to purchase the line or any portion of it within a certain period. After some discussion it was decided to add the following s:th reason to the request to the Rural Dis- trict Council. " (5) It the power of making the rail- way is obtained the Parish Council of Stockton Heath should have the right to purchase the railway in their parish at the end of 10 Tian and any flee years after- wards." The roquest in its amended imin was unanimouely passed.</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON " WORDSWORTH." -,1*3 ,rtnl At the meeting of the Warrington Literary and Philosophical Society on Mor.day evening, Dr Naden delivered an appreciative and thoughtful paper on " Wordsworth." In dealing with the young days of the poet, the lecturer said Wordsworth's early life was not quite ordinary according to modern standards. He was sent to school when ten years old and eejsved a liberty quite at variance with the tenets of strict disciplinarians. His prescribed studies were not arduous, and he did not appear to have supplemented them by self-imposed tasks. He reed anything that came his way without supervision. He stayed out late at night, or all night if he pleased, and frequently rose early enough to ramble from five to ten miles before school began at six. He was physicilly strong, happy, boisterous, athletic, out on the fells at night snaring woodcocks, climbing rocky precipices to plunder ravens' nests, boating, skating, riding, flying kites, fishing. " Happy time it was indeed for all of us, he wrote. " For me it was a time of rapture.' There was nothing morbid or unhealthy</p>
        <p>in him. It was well to accentuate that side of his youth, because they must notice another side—dreamy, mystical, imaginative—as uncommon as it was interest- ing, the memory of which inspired one of the greatest poems of the century. One phase of his education was singularly happy for him. No one seemed to have worried him about reading good literature to improve his mind or tried to cultivate his taste by suggesting love and admiration for natural scenery, or directed his attention to fashiocable intellectual or artistic crazes. He was left free to follow his bent, and he unconsciously developed the natural instincts with which he was so liberally endowed. The most extraordinary aspect of his youthful mind was his Idealism. They were all now familiar with the idea that matter might he only a phantasm and mind the only reality. Carlyle and Goethe had popularised the idea. It was, however, one thing to have an intellectual idea, play with it, discuss it, neither accept nor reject it,but quite:another to realize in one's own experience that it was true. In early life Wordsworth's delight in nature was mainly sensuous, form end colour holding him captive, and unless he had , misinterpreted him, he felt the unity between nature ' and man to be much more than mere government by the same physical laws. There was for him something approaching to reciprocal emotion, thought and love. He studied her moods and thoughts, and feelings, and portrayed them in a new way with a new meaning and with the delight of a lover for his mistress. There sr as ample evidence that Wordswoeth's natural religion was not more intellectual assent, but that it entered into hie very being. For practical purposes it was not important that any religion should be true. He believed in the essential unity of man, beast, and nature in all its forms ; and how that belief affected his con- duct might be traced in many of his poems. If, there- fore, Wordswortlis idea of man's relation to external objects was such that even hazels should not be ruth- lessly destroyed, nor animals subjected to pain for mere sport, they might hare some conception of his idea of man's relation to man. From early life he held definite opinions on that subject. long before his acquaintance with the turmoil of the French Revolution. He felt that in every social community no man oould live successfully without honest useful labour for the public good, and that neither wealth nor other consideration WM sufficient excuse for an idle, useless life. He had no admiration for the man whole vrhose thou ffits were devoted to schemes for the transfer of property from one pocket to another without any service useful to the cemmunity being rendered. Neither did he believe that the world Was made for an aristocracy who should treat all plebians as sponges to be squeezed for their benefit. He believed in an aristocracy, but many of its members were to be found among the poor and humble. Were not some of the ableet Roman Emperors born peasants ? It was a curious question whether man was not naturally a tyrant and only kept from being tyrannical by the pressure from without. Even the older civiliza- tions always feared their slaves might rise in revolt,and how that fear must have modified the treatment of slaves ! They scarcely realised the restraint from with- out habitually imposed on their baser passions and he (the speaker) sometimes wondered how it was that law and public opinion were so much more righteous than average individual rectitude. In that case the whole was not only equal in quantity to the sum of the parts but was of better quality. Was it that revealed com- mands were obeyed as of supernatural origin ; or was it that the naturally righteous few succeeded in imposing restraint on the many ? He believed the secret to be this—that no man was willing to allow others the same liberty which he thought ho might claim for himself. It might do for him to have certain liberty,but the man over the way ? Oh,no one could trust him ! Hence, as lawgivers they rose above their natural selves, and law and public opinion supplied sufficient pressure to supple- ment their defective principles. When they had all de- cided to grant to others the liberty or licence they would like for themiielves, then either the millennium or rever- sion to savagery would not be far off. These reflections had been suggested by the restraints which Wordsworth habitually imposed on himself. No man was subject to more scathing criticism, yet he did not reply, even when he could have crushed his oppon- ent. The only result of the criticism was to snake him examine himself and his work more carefully. Ho was fond of society, of life, of travel ; but he felt that he had a great work to do, and unless greatly sinning he must be a c mseerated spirit. How could a man who felt that and held his opinions he other than he was ? "It is an awful truth," he writes, *. that there neither is nor can he any genuine enjoyment of poetry among nineteen out of twenty of those persons who live, or wish to live, in the broad light of the world—among those who either are or arc striving to make themselves people of con- sideration in seciety. 'This is a truth, and an awful one, because to be incapable of a feeling of poetry in any sense of the word is to be without levee of human nature and reverence for God." He was one of those men who</p>
        <p>in earlier ages woula. have founded .a.religion• There</p>
        <p>were many, if not all, the characteristics necessary for such an office. His idealism, enthusiasm, cosmopolitan love of man, rigid virtue, tiv power of attracting dig. cipleg—few but fit—and implicit faith in his own powers were the things they expected in a great religious teacher.</p>
        <p>In spite of the earnestness, intensity, and restraint he could sympathize with all sorts and conditions of men. Benjamin, tho waggoner, who would stop too long at the wayside inns, was evidently a favourite, and the poet had given them a vigorous description of the village merry-making, at which Benjamin was tempted to exceed the limits of prudence and sobriety. There was no time to speak of Wordsworth as a patriot. Hia patriotism, however, did not begin and end with " Rule &amp;Mania," When the Government of hi 3 time engaged in wars he did not approve ; he did not mind saying so. And yet he was a patriot. One of the best ways to realize what the people of this country thought and felt during the Napoleonic wars was to read his sonnets The beauties of Wordsworth were innumerable. His. descriptions were exact and sympathetic. His thought was suggestive. His character was forceful and stimu- lating and he bad left them a heritage " undefiled and that fadetla not away." kApplause.) The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON, who presided, regretted the small attendance, but said it was no doubt owing to the dissipations of a dissipated town that were now in full swing to the exclusion of all else. He was, how- ever, proud of the fact that they could raise from the town those who could give them papers. He did not think he had ever listened to a paper which contained so many apt quotations from the works of the poet. It was by no means clear to the ordinary reader what the poet's religious views were, but they might venture to say that his intense love for nature must not be mis- taken into making him in any sense create a divinity of nature. The intense reverence for every living thing had been shared by others of whose religious opinions there was no doubt at all. Councillor BENNETT proposed a vote of thanks to Dr. Naden for his excellent paper,and alluded to* both Wordsworth and Browning. Ho said that some people thought Browning extremely obscure. There was a certain amount of truth in the assertion, though he thought the i iea was very much exaggerated. Anyone who tackled Browning would find the obscurities more apparent than real. In the same way some were de- terred from a study of Wordsworth bacause they thought him tame and uninteresting, but if they read him in the right way they would find some view or ides that was simply beautiful. Both deserved to b 3 much more widely studied, and the way in which people fought shy of them was a little unfair to both. Major BucEroN seconded, and Mr May supported, the latter remarking that War doworth very seldom rose above what they might cad measured prose, and that was why they found it so wearisome to read through a lengthy poem. Mr J RYMER YOUNG rather sympathised with Mr May</p>
      </div>
