      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>for Pale People.</head>
        <p>for Pale People.</p>
        <p>RtA REMARKABLE instance of the ruinous effects eiiikkig Thanks ! of Anocmia upon the constitution of young women comes to hand from a young lady living in Tranter's Row, Tqweester, Northamptonshire. On a recent occasion Miss Ellen Skinner gave the following account of her long fight with ill-health, to a well-known pressman. She explained: "For twelve years I have suffered the greatest misery, owing to continual ill-health caused by Anxmia. I might say that from the age of sixteen I did not know what it was to feel well and happy. During this long tune I consulted many excellent doctors, but it all seemed to no purpose, and, instead of improving, I gradually became weaker. I used to spit up blood, and had severe pains in my chest and bead, so violent at times that I could scarcely keep my eyes open. I suffered from shortness of breath, and fearful perspirations at night brought me so languid and weak that it seemed trial to move about. I was so weak that my mother had to lift me in and out of bed for Iver ten years. To go upstairs was a task quite beyond me; in fact, I could not manage get downstairs without help." "One would hardly credit such a statement to see you now," remarked the interviewer.</p>
        <p>'How has this wonderful reformation come about?" "Just before Easter, 1999, a little book came by post, and in it I read of wonderful cures effected by Dr. 'Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. It seemed like a ray of hope, and I resolved to give the pills a trial. I began to feel slightly better from the first. I knew I was deriving bmeflt as I continued with Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, for I left off spitting blood and the sharp pains in my chest and head stopped. In June I was able to walk downstairs with the help of a stick, and a week later I astonished the village by attending church in a bath-chair. People could hardly believe their eyes, and said I was a living wonder. Daring the service thanks were returned for my recovery. 'Bo the improvement continued until I felt a different person. Now I fear no exertion, and look after my father's house, even running upstairs without being distressed. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have saved my life and done for me what no doctor's medicine could. I am truly thankful that I have overcome the terrible illness that rendered me a burden to everyone for years, and I am now looking forward to the enjoyment of life favoin ecl with the best of health." Sold by Dr. Williams' Medicine CompAy, Holborn Viaduct, Lotulon; Price, 2s. 9d.; Six Boxes, 13s. 9d.: also by all Chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>motives and absolute confidence in your personal honour. If you still think it necessary to persist in your resignation, I shall accept it with regret, and, if I do not hear from you to the contrary in the course of this weak, I shall assume that your decision is final, Believe me, Dear Mr. Bennett, to be ' Yours Faithfully, H. PLOBBiITS, Mayor.</p>
        <p>Market Gate Chambers, Warrington. 11th January, 1901.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Mayor, lam in receipt of your letter. I think the reasons which I give for resigning my position as Mayor's Auditor are sufficient, and I can only leave the public to judge as to the manner in which you have supported ma in the discharge of my duties. Permit me to point out that the report was ad- dressed "To the Mayor, Aldermen, and Burgesses of the Borough of Warrington." It is not neces- sary to again discuss that report in detail ; but I may say that I abide by all that it contains. I appreciate the compliment which you are good enough to pay to the integrity of my motives, and I willingly reciprocate the same; but I regret to add that, under present circumstances, I do not see how I can altar my decision.--Faithfully yours, ARTHUR. BENNETT.</p>
        <p>To His Worship, The Mayor of Warrington. P..—As the matter is of public interest, I pro- pose to send a copy of this correspondence to the press.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>COUNCILLOR A. J. JOLLEY APPOINTED</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR A. J. JOLLEY APPOINTED</p>
        <p>Councillor Arthur Bennett having finally decided to resign his position as Mayor's auditor, the duty devolved upon the Mayor to appoint a successor. On Saturday he wrote to Councillor A J Jolley, offering him the appointment, and on Monday, a repl3 was received from Mr Jolley agreeing to ac- tept the position. The correspondence which passed between the Mayor and his newly-appointed audi- tor was as follows "Town Ilan, Warrington. January 12th, 1901' Dear Councillor A J Jolley,—l beg to offer you the appointment of Mayor's auditor. If you can see your way to accept the office you will conter upon me a personal favour, which I shall warmly appre- ciate, and at the same time, you will add to the ob- ligation, whichyou have for a long time laid upon the town and district for your In ig and distin- guished public services.—Hoping to have your ' favourable reply, believe me, faithfully, H. Roberts, May or." "59, Marsh House-lane, Warrington, January, 14th, 1901. Dear Mr Mayor,—Permit me fir,t to acknowledge the goodwill whic prompted the too flattering ter.ns of your letter of the 12th inst. Secondly, if you are of opinion, in view of the re- grettable imputations which have been so unwar- rantably cast upon the Corporation, that my accep- tance of the office would be for the common good, I will undertake the duties of Mayor's auditor.— Sincerely yours, Alf. J. Jolley." A FREE VETERINARY BOOK.</p>
        <p>The proprietors of Candy's Fluid, of 14, aiwell-road, London, E C. are, we understand posting to all appli- cants, free of charge, a most useful book containing in structions from Veterinary Surge )ns of the hitiest s.anding for the treatment and cure of ailments and- complaints affecting Horscs, Dogs, Cats, Cattle, Sheep, Pigs, Rabbits. Poultry,Figeons, Cage Birds,Gold Fish Szr.). Candy's Fluid is really a magical remedy for suffering animals, and this book will be found extremely We recommend all our readers to write for it to Condy's Fluid Works, 14, Goswell-road, London, B.C. N.) charge is made for the Book or for Postage.</p>
        <p>,Accident at a Local Theatre—A narrow Escape. An accident which allot have had serious con- sequences, occurred at the Theatre Royal, Scotland- road at 2.40 on Wednesday afternoon, it appears that a youth named David Dunn, who resides at Sankey Bridges, and two other men in the employ of Mr Dell, Sank, y-street, were engaged in paint- ing the ceiling of the building. For this purpose they were standing on a plank, when suddenly, one of the poles supporting it, snapped, and the men were precipitated below. Dunn fell in- to the pit, a distance of 30 feet, but fortunately his fall was broken by the balcony or he might have been very seriously injured. As it was, he sustained a severe scalp wound, and be was removed to the Infirmary. After the wound had been dressed, he was allowed to go home. The other men fell into the gallery, a aistance of 16 , feet, but did not sustain any injury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>HARRIERS.</head>
        <p>HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>Runcorn v. Halton View.</p>
        <p>••-••—••• -• -••••••—•• •-•-• Tte fixture on Saturday last was an inter-eltib tun with the Halton View Harriers at Willies, hut at the last monthly mesting,which was held on January 7th, it was decided to cancel this fixture, as the Runcorn club hae had some strong clubs to compete against the last few works. As this has caused a great amount of staleness amongst the runners, a rest on Saturday was unani- mously decided on. A few of those who die hard in- dulged in a pleasant jaunt through the country finishing up with the mile break, %n which Leyland scrambled home a yard in front of Mathews. :Sankey v. Crewe.</p>
        <p>These clubs met at Sankey oa Saturday to decide their return Inter-club race. More than ordinary interest was centred in the event, as Crewe hold an unbeaten record for this season. Sankey were without the services of two of their prominent counters, T Devlin and It Throssell, whilst the visitors wera without Chester's. It was decided to count the first six of each team. Twelve of the visitors turned out to do battle against sixteen of the home team. Trail was laid over seven miles of heavy country by Messrs Cuoper,and Roberts,by way of Bold estate,and returning on tisJ I",,..iketh side. The pack started off at a rattling pace, keeping well together until about half the journey was treverseclovhen J Morris and T Liessock took the lead,followed by J Riley,sen., Peach, and J Riley, jun., J Morris, however, was successful in maintaining his lead, acid won a fine race by about tan yards from T Hossock, T P Peach coming third, Crewe thu3 having the first three men home.—The following are the positions of the counters of each team :,—Crewe J Morris, 1; T Hossock, 2; T P Peach, 3 ; Stubbs, 6; II KolsalJ, 8; A 133wyer, 9 ; total, 29 points. Sankey ; J Riley, jun.;4; J Riley,seo.,s ; J Wilding, 7; E Hilto3,lo ; G Eastwood, 12 ; E Davis, 14; total, 52 points. Crewe thus won easily by. 23 point. Alter the run the visitors were entertained to tea,follo wed by an enjoyable soda.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Orford Harriers.</head>
        <p>Orford Harriers.</p>
        <p>On Saturday 12 members of the Warrington Orford Barriers had an enj..yable run over about 6 miles of country. One runner while getting over a fence sprained his ankle. To-day they expect Crewe Harrier/ as visitors.</p>
