        <p>Correspondence between the Mayor and Uouncillor</p>
        <p>Bennett.</p>
        <p>As a result of what transpired during the recent di cussion oa the auditors' report, Mr C )uncill )r Beanett, the Mayor's auditor, has sent in his resig- nation of that office to tha Mayor. It is also steed on good authority that Mr Bolton, one of the elec- tive auditors, whose term of office expires in a month, will not seek re-appointment. Mr Bolton has been an elective aultor for eleven years.</p>
        <p>The Borough Audit. w We understand that the following correspon- dence has passed between Councillor Bennett and the Mayor. Market Gate Chambers, Warrington, oth Jan., 1001. _ _</p>
        <p>DEAR Mn. MAYOR, After what has transpired at the last meetings of the General Purposes Committee and the Council, I feel it necessary to write to you at once in refer- ence to the Borough Audit. The law upon the question is notoriously in- definite and.:unsatisfactory, and Borough Auditors can only do their work under very difficult circum- stances, in accordance with the dictates of their individual judgment,a.nd with a due tegard for what they believe to be the rights of the ratepayerA. During the seven years in which I have had the honour to be Mayor's Auditor, I have consistently endeavoured to act in this spirit, and have not spared either time or trouble in the execution of my task: and, when I accepted the re-appointment at your hands, I felt that, however we might differ in matters of detail, I might rely upon you to support me in discharging the responsible duties of the office-to the best of my ability and with fidelity and independence. To hold the position on any other terms is impos- sible to any self-n-tspecting professional accountant; and, in face of your recent attitude upon the sub- ject, I have not any alt ernative but to tender my resignation.—Faith fully yours, ARTHUR BENNETT. To His Worship the Mayor of Warrin4ton.</p>
        <p>Town Hall, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Dear, Mr Bennett,</head>
        <p>Dear, Mr Bennett,</p>
        <p>lam in receipt of yours of even date. I regret that you so old resign your posit ion of Mayor's Auditor for what are to my mind such inadequate reasons. You are doing me a serious injustice if you sugg-st that, I e .withheld from you any sup- port nec-ssary to enable you td discharge the duties of the office in question. I may remind you that I stated,at the General Purposes Committee Meeting, that I bad not previously seen the Auditors' Report and, if you considered its publication of such vital importance, it was your duty as Mayor's Auditor to have placed all the facts before me months ago, and to have asked for my decision thereupon. You did not do so, anditheref ore no responsibility rests upon me. What Iso strongly iesent is the fact that the Auditors have made statements in their report, which, in the case of the Middleton Trust, consti- tuted an expression of opinion both uncalled for and Unjustifiable, and involving the Museum Com- mittee, of which you are a member, and to whose action in this question you have been a party, in a liabi.ity to censure from their fellow- townsmen, for an act which was prompted by the purest motives, done under legal sanc- tion, and since approved by the Authority to whom the Committee is responsible In the matter of Corporation expenditure on deputations' the statement contained in the report was not sup- ported by any evidence whatever, either in the re- port itself or by you personally, and, as a matter of fact, is not a correct statement. Surely you do not expect that the members of our Corporation, who,in the main, a; aoy ra e, are as high-minded, as cap- able, end as honest as their fellows outside tue Council, should sit down add accept with absolute placidity, a criticism which has proved to ba in the main uojust and uncalled for? In this discussion I have not said a word reflect- ing upon you in your personal capacity. I have the highest opinion of the integrity and purity of your</p>
