      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EXAM IN ER ,</head>
        <p>THE EXAM IN ER ,</p>
        <p>JANUARY 18th 1901</p>
        <p>Blaming the Right Ir is oil very well to Persons. heap up blame upon the heads of working-men for every conceivable danger that threatens British industries, but the public should be quite sure that they are blaming the right persons, and it does not appear to us that they have taken the trouble to think out the matter correctly. The competitions with which we are threatened do not depend upon output or even price, as far as price is due to wages. The complaints which are cur- rent may be resolved into two or three—deficiency of enterprise abroad, and lack of skill at home. Our commercial machinery is defective, it is said. We do not send out sufficient commercial travel- lers, and they are unversed in foreign languages. We open fewer agencies than we ought to do, and our price-lists are printed in English and in English moneys. Parts of the world where British goods used to be paramount are now deluged with German and American products. Our commercial training at home lacks practical elements. Our artisans lack technical training. What is there, in all these defects, to direct blame upon working- men? Nothing whatever. But it is said that we import into this country goods wholly or partly manufactured which ought to be produced at home. Why they are not produced is what the public wants to know, and it is no explanation to say that foreign workmen work longer hours and are not banded together in trade unioes. America is a great competitor in many articles, and wages are higher in America than in this country in all the skilled and in.many of the unskilled trades, whilst living is cheaper, except in the matter of house- rent andclothes. The engineering articles which enter so largely into arguments about competition are remarkable for two features—they are lighter, and they are produced more by labour-saving machinery than similar articles are with us at home. If home customers will have more durable and substantial articles, no blame can attach to working-men, and they are not making war against machinery that we are aware of, because the fact is patent that more of it is used in all engineering shops. If we pass to another point—the individual productiveness of the operative in all branches of industry—there is no reliable evidence to show that it is higher abroad than it is at home,and there is good ground for asserting that the workmen of the Continent who work longer hours do not turn out more work in a week. On this latter point,in tex- tile trades, the evidence in favour of the shorter hours' production is overwhelming. What, then, is the fault of the British workman relative to threatened competition ? Is he to blame because German steamships steam to America faster than our own? Can we say that he iis responsible for the inferior speed of our railways ? Is he to be censured for the lack of energy and development I i Surely the d indiscrimin- ate particularly n electricalcensure oafpapnliyanccle ? ass s is unjust, of the working class. The responsibility for apathy, sloth, and easy-going trade must rest on the right shoulders. It is said that strikes drive away trade. Well, legislation to avert strikes has had some effect in reducing their num- ber, and if it be necessary in the general interests of the country to prevent strikes, the law must be made more drastic, and arbitration be rendered compulsory. Opposition to this course comes, at present, from masters as well as men. The latter want to be free of State interference, and they give</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>the Board of Trade an unpleasant experience when it seeks to interfere. The net result of this examination is this—that we cannot attribute increasing competition abroad and at home to the men who produce the articles. An impartial judgment is that our enterprise needs to be quickened, and to be ,more intelligent. Manufac- turers must bestir themselves, and the general community must see that our education is better and our science more piactical.</p>
        <p>EVERY Liberal wishes to see Yearning for a Settlement, a settlement in South Africa, and he is ready to discuss any reasonable plan. The news that DE WET has flogged and then shot a British subject who was the bearer of peace overtures comes to us on the authority of Lord KITCHENER, or it would not be credited. If there be any room for doubt as to the authenticity of the incident, we shall all be glad to feel the doubt. But it does not make us very hopeful as to tho result of any moderate settcle- ment that could be proposed by Lord Krmixxxrt himself. The burghers who want a cessation of the war cannot make their influence felt upon the Boer commanders in the field. They see the folly of further resistance as much as we do, and they lament the ruin which is coming upon the country overrun by the contending armies. Unfortunately, they seem to be powerless. Mr. KRUGER'S influence is against them, as far as it can reach them. The prolonga- tion of the war is believed to be the only means of obtaining that for which it was originated—inde- pendence. Are the British people willing to give the Boers independence? Mr. COURTNEY, whose candour is as great as his sincerity is undoubted, is unable to go so far as this. The nation wants he says, "British supremacy throughout South Africa, in a fashiOn to secure equal justice to all white inhabitants and to prevent a recurrence of the present contest." It seems to us that Dx WET and the militant party in the Boer country would reject this solution as well as any other put in a cruder form. Controlling foreign affairs and dis- armament would, Mr. COURTNEY thinks, be suffi- cient to avert a recurrence of present troubles. The Boers have never put into words any other basis of settlement, so far as we know an "independence," and we doubt whether they would assent to an armistice to dis- cuss any other. If at any time they were disposed to think of terms, they could have an armistice by asking forhit. Mr KRUGER knows better.now than he did what are the real conditions of the fighting forces, and what are the prospects of success for his countrymen. The "blood and tears" of his countrymen figured in the manifesto he issued after Paardeberg, when he pleaded for independ- ence and was denied it. Are we ready to give now after all our sacrifices, what we refused then ? No. E$ en Mr CounTxxx declares for "British suprem- acy," which is a denial of independence. It did not exist before the war. It must, in some form or another, be established as the result of the war. The situation is in many respects peculiar. There is no established authority to which the Boers in the field will listen. It is not certain they would stop the war if there were to come intimations from Mr. lianonn tkat th;s was the right policy, and orders from him to make the best terms possible and lay down their arms. As long as he refrains from efforts to stop the war, it looks as it the plans of the burghers would fail as well as our own. Yet we are all yearning for a settlement, and to find some other solution than that of extermination. We could wish there were clear lights as to some way out. Mr Coußni-EY suggests an effort. We agree with him that it should be made. The basis must be British supremacy. Would Mr KRUGER accept that rs a first principle? If he would something would be gained. We could wish it were clearer that Mr STEYN, DE WET, and BOTHA would accept it. Their disposition is "independence or death" and, obviously, nothing can be done with combatants who assume this desperate attitude.</p>
        <p>11 The Ragged School, as it was originally organ- ised, is certainly a thing of the past ; but it does not necessarily follow, as a writer in the current magazine of toe Charity Organization Society seems to suggest, that its disappearance is a fact to be regretted. Tne Ragged Schools were founded by private benevolence tor the benefit of a specirl. class. The State having since been roused to a sense of its duty towards all classes in the matter of providing educational facilities, the particular need passes away. Nevertheless, the history of Rauged Schools will always remain a very credit • abre chapter in the records of British philanthropy. They w ere really an off-shoot from Sunday-schools, and the genuineness of the movement is apparent from the fact that the most prominent organisers were almost entirely men of humble rank, among whom the name of the Portsmouth cobbler, John Pounds, is worthy of lasting honour. They were atilt struggling institutions, ill-funded and ill- equipped, in the early 'forties of last century, when thepatronage of the late Earl Shaftesbury, and in, perhaps, a greater degree that of Charles Dickens, drew public attention to the good work which was being done, and even forced the claims of the movement upon the Government of the day and secured a grudging and temporary grant. The Education Act, nowever, swept aside the special necessity for these schools, which became rapidly merged in the elementary schools of the land. Thus, though the Ragged School Union still con- tinues a useful work, its responsibilities are, through the interposition of the State, of a very different kindfrom those its founders had in view.</p>
        <p>The Vanderbilt wedding in New York is a melancholy story of vulgarity. We are told how corps of florists arrived to clacorate the church in the morning, and how one idea—a floral horseshoe over the entrance to the church—cost 5,000d01. to erect. The entire description of the wedding,even when reduced to the modified langlage of the English press, palls in its splendeur. It is strange that such a ceremony should be performed in the name of; religion, and with references to "The Voice that breathed o'er Eden," as if simplicity and not artificiality were to be the keynote. Stranger still is the desire of the family, having ex- cited public attention hy this ostentious display, to shroud the honeymoon tour in mystery. It is said that the destination of the bride and bridegroom was kept a profound secret because of the journalists, and the journalists appreciating the compliment, are doing their ut• most to follow the ti,.-k. It is tolerably certain that if the Vanderbilts Jid not enjoy publicity they would have taken care to avoid it earlier in life. The pleasure, however, carries with it a severe punishment. If the rich Americans like to be talked about eternally,it must follow that, the talk will not always be flattering. Sweets and sours must be mixed. The consequence is that the American Press has a licence which we may hope will never be tolerated in our own country. There is practically no libel law, and no limit to abuse—except regard for the circulation of tha newspapers. --- The Warrington Board of Guardians has just been reminded of the circular which was issued by the Local Government Board in August last relat- ing to poor law administration and aged and deserving poor. This precious document was issued at the time when rumours wore in the air of an approaching general election and there were not a few who were disposed to conclude that the circular had been sent round more with an eye of catching</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>votes rather than any very strong desire on the part of the head authority to improve the lot of the poor who come under the care of the Board of Guardians. It now appears that the Local Government Board is desirous of ascertaining the result of the consideration of the circular, and the Warrington Board along with others in the country has been asked to send in a report on the subject. The Clerk of the Warrington Board has an easy task. The circular received very brief consideration at Warrington, for if we remember rightly, it was dismissed with the suggestive remark that the recommendations had been already carried out by the Warrington Board. For many years prior to the receipt of the circular the main suggestions have been in active operation in the Warrington Union and the Local Govern- ment Board is theref.re just a trifle late in the day The Clerk has now drawn up a reply incorporating these facts. One would have thought that the central authority would have been cognisant of them, seeing that all the schemes adopted have received their approval.</p>
        <p>--- Anti-vaccinationists who have had the temerity to apply to the Warrington Bench of magistrates fo exemption orders have recently received a very Warm reception. A sensitive person wouldoer- tainly feel that he had been insulted. An appli- cation made on Thursday morning may be taken as a case in point. The, applicant was bluntly told that the application was prompted by "a desire for notoriety ;" that such people " did not care a rap for their children," and such mild words as " stupidity " and " ignorance " were freely-inter- larded with the magistrates' condemnation. It can hardly be said that this browbeat- ing has much effect on the applicant or is calculated to add to the dignity of the Bench. Either the applicant has a right to make a con- scientious objection to vaccination or he has not. If the law permits him then it is for the magis- trates to carry out their duties. The law may seem foolish and unnecessary,but that is no reason Why an applicant should not be able to appear before the magistrates without receiving a castiga- tion for doing what an Act of Parliament empowers him to do.</p>
        <p>Every householder is interested in the price of Dailk and any movement calculated to increase the Price of that wholesome necessary commodity, de- bands attention. Last week a large meeting of iarmers was held at the Corn Exchange, Warring- ton, and a resolution was unanimously. passed to join the Cheshire Milk Producers' Association with a committee at Warrington to manage the work of the district. The chief object for this combina- tion amongst dairy farmers was stated with great frankness to be that of raising the selling price of milk, it may therefore be taken for granted that the householder in the town will not view with a friendly eye the progress of the Milk Producers' Association. In fact, the quality of the milk supplied is sometimes of such , a" blueness" as to give grounds for the house- , holders' conviction that the price already paid is quite enough. If along with an increase in the price of milk, a rich quality will be guaranteed, there will be less ground for complaint. We quite ' agree with the remark of Mr Sadler, made at the meeting alluded to,that the question of adulteration will never be satisfactorily settled until the milk is collected in factories, cooled properly, filtered and made free fro si germs sad sold on the butter fat test. Any steps which are taken by the Association in this direction are to be wel- comed. Our continental neighbours have• done much in this way and if the British milk producer desires to hold his present supreme position and keep out the " foreigner " he will not only have to consider the amount of profit to be obtained, but attend to precautions which are dictated by the , laws of health and common-sense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT. ---- A Provincial Letter.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. ---- A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>L.</p>
        <p>The good St. Valentine, according to report, has for some years past been in apparent need of a aultuulant ; but whether he will find it such to hear members of Parliament saying or singing, on the fourteenth of February, "It is our opening day," znay be qnestioned. Those who have ben described by high authority, "as our heavy-minded legisla- tors," generally, and the present Premier in particu- lar, would stoutly disclaim the "fixture " for the opening of Parliament as having any relation to the poetic idea. But all the same, those who still in- dulge in honouring St. Valentine after the usual fashion, will have to address their missives to bachelor M.P.'s, at St, Stephen's, Westminster, for the memorable fourteenth ; while others of a less sentimental and more practical turn of mind, will be hetrtily wishing that the first Session of the New Century may be neither wholly a war, or an Irish session, but one in which social and domestic questions may be dealt with, affecting the interests of al. and of the English provinces especially. The Pure Beer question bids fair to occupy a prominent Place in the earlier part of the Session, but there is tot much danger of the House of Commons being 'Captured" by the Chaplin—Quilter—Sewell—Read agitation. Something that savours less of class in- terest than this will no doubt be devised.</p>
        <p>How very slow our leg:slators have been in reforming the Company laws may be judged from a glance at the latest report of the Inspector- General in Companies Liquidation, which only brings us to the end of the year 1899. The amount of Capital involved in the liquidation of joint stock companies during eight years has been nearly 404 millions sterling, of which no less than 209 millions have been subscribed by the public. What may be described as the sum paid by the public in cash to companies declared insolvent in 1899 millions. All this amounted' vast in v round figures to nearly 331 fortune, of course, ir_ay not have been completely lost to the investors Many of the companies have been wound up voluntarily and reconstructed, and in a number of cases something has been saved from the general wreckage. But it has been estim- ated that the direct los to the .public through the failure of joint stock companies is something like twelve millions a year. It is hoped that the Com- panies Act which has just come into operation may , put a stop to the flotation of bogus i companies, and Promote honesty in business. This s assuredly the only way in which the nation can hope to succeed ?n. trade. The tendency to form private concerns Into public companies is increasing,and great trusts i are being established. The surplus money n the hands of investors can be turned to good purpose, Profitable to themselves and to the nation, if it flows in the right channel; and this is the aim and object of the new enactment which seems calcu- lat d to save the public from themselves to the tune 'of millions annually.</p>
        <p>That the Ministry have now Wren the right Conr6e in establishing a tribunal to inquire into the recent exceptional sickness and death attributed to beer-poisoning will be generally admitted. The Opinion of medical experts has ben expressed again and again that the dPaths were due to the drinking of poisoned beer, and the Royal Commission which has just been formed will inquire whether the sick- ness and death have been due to arsenic in the 'national beverage, or in any othe r articles of drink or food, and if so how it got there. Should the Poisoning be traced to arsenic, there will be a fur- ther report as to the precautions by which the dan- ge Can be avoided in future. Starting with a practical and sensible plan of operations before' them, the investigations of the Commissioners will be followed with the greatest interest in all those parts of the provinces that have suffered, and it must be generally hoped that the results will Prove satisfactory. It is not always possible to aPorove the composition of Royal Commissions, but in the present instance little or no exception has been taken in that respect. The members are well-known,men in whose judgment and ability the Public can repose absolute confidence. When the obscure cau,e of so much sickness and death has been properly traced, the Government will know how to deal with the evil, and until then the pub- lic should reserve their apportionment of the blame. It is, of course, possible that in the course of the inquiry other drink and food may be found to contain certain injurious constituents</p>
        <p>- The population of England and Wales in 1891 Was found to be twenty-nine millions, and it is estimated that it will be thirty-two millions when the census is taken this year. Just over fourteen of the 29 millions were male, and nearly fifteen xmllions were female, the excess of the latter being</p>
        <p>886,723. This time the disparity will be greater, because of the unusually large number of men of the army and navy being away. It takes the Registrar-General two or three years to complete his analysis of the census, and he begins in earnest to prepare for the ensuing enumeration about eighteen months in ad- vance. The registrars in the country have already had several communications from their chief, as well as the letter of instructions. They have to engage a sufficient number of qualified enumerators, and already are receiving applications from clerks and others who are anxious to be employed as enumerators. The pay is a guinea, and 3s 6d for every hundred names, or fraction of a hundred above 400. All the returns must be checked by local registrars,and then forwarded to the Registrar- General.</p>
        <p>Electric tramway schemes are much in evidence in the provinces, and Sunderland has just been showing to other places the profits that are to be made out of them. It was reported at the last meeting of the Town Council there that from one section that had been running since August and another since the end of November the receipts, which had been previously estimated at 103 a mile, had bean Is 6d, and chat there was a netiprofit of more than three thousand pounds! As this is far better than had been anticipated, the Corporation have decided to now spend £20,000 in extensions, making the total amount invested £250,000, which is expected to produce ten thou- sand pounds a year.</p>
        <p>Judge Owen thinks that South Wales will soon be in a position to form a ring" in law-tankers. Nearly all the members of the South Wales Circuit, he declares, are either members of Parliament or "going to be." Lawyer-made law may be expected to be good for the lawyers, but whether it is to all others may well be questioned. It has long been felt that there are sadly too many lawyers in Par- liament, and a lawyer, who is a Judge! to boot, appears to feel it as well as do laymen.</p>
        <p>For the year 1900 the total trade of the United Kingdom, including imports, exports, and re- exports, has been eight hundred and seventy-eight millions sterling, or an increase of some sixty-four millions compared with the preceding year. Imports have increased thirty-eight and a half millions on the year, and five and three-quarter ' millions on the month. Exports are twenty-seven millions better on the year, and a million and a half on the month. Re-exports alone have decreased, to the extent of nearly two millions. But this is a favourable sign, since it is an evidence that we are buying less from foreign countries to retail in dis- tant markets, and are selling more of our own manufactures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>IMPORTANT BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held in the Board Room at the Workhouse, on Wednes- day morning. Mr. A J Jolley presided, and there were also present: Mrs Clay,Mrs Edelsten,Rev T R Matthews, Dr. Patterson, Messrs. T H Ashcroft, William Hickman, A Jolley, T B Malkin, S Davies, T Halton, 11 Thornton, William Houghton, A Brooks, J Chake' E 1.. Bent, G Pilling, J Billings, J C Sutton (clerk), S Sutton (assis- tant clerk), CJII Ward (master)•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LARGE INCREASE IN EXPENDITURE.</head>
        <p>LARGE INCREASE IN EXPENDITURE.</p>
        <p>------- - The CLERK stated that the accounts for the Padgate Schools and the Workhouse for the quarter showed a total increase of £405 as compared witn the correspond ing period of last year. Mr. HICKMAN : How do you account for that ? The CLERK said it rose from internal causes. Nothing had been done except what had been ordered by the Board. Mr. WARD explained that there had been additional expenditure on water in connection with the boilAs and coal. The ASSISTANT CLERK remarked that the cost of coal was about four times as much. Mr. HICKMAN : And there has been a large increase in the number of inmates. The CLERK said the total accounts exceeded £4,000. They had never come to that sum before except when the County Rates had been paid. The CHAIRMAN said the increase the Clerk had alluded to did not include salaries. There was an increase of salaries as well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>.4ggiti-ATE-ACCOMMODATION NEEDED FOR FEMALE LUNATICS.</head>
        <p>.4ggiti-ATE-ACCOMMODATION NEEDED FOR FEMALE LUNATICS.</p>
        <p>Dr BRAIDE repOr-t-e-c-Ctl-ta-t nothing bad yet bean done to provide separate accommodation for the female luna- tics apart from the other patients and he recommended that the old infirmary be structurally altered to find such accommodation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>FITE-1711-0-1:6-SED ALTERATIONS AT THE WORKHOUSE.</head>
        <p>FITE-1711-0-1:6-SED ALTERATIONS AT THE WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from tne Local Government Board approving of the plans relating to the proposed altera- tions at the Warrington Union Workhouse. The Board would address a further communication to the Guar- dians as to the steps to be taken preliminary to the issue of the necessary order authorising the expenditure of a sum not exceeding £lOO3 on the work. If the Guar diens decided to borrow the money to defray the cost of the work thejßoard considered the loan should be:repaid in 20 years. THE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD'S CIRCULAR. REPLY OF THE WARRINGTON GUARDIANS. A. letter was road from the Local Government Board asking to be informed as to the result of the considera- tion by the Guardians of the Board's circular of the 4th August relatink to Poor Law administration and aged and deserving poor. That circular pressed upon the Guardians three points. (1) That the children should be removed from the Workhouse. (2) That where out- relief is granted to aged deserving persons it should be adequate in amount. (3) That a special class of aged deserving inmates of the Workhouse should be Greeted to whom special privileges should be given. Mr Fust asked if the Guardians would furnish a short report. The CLERK, in his reply, said that (1) For many years prior to such circular, indoor poor children chargeable to the Union had at a reasonable period following their admission, been removed from the Union Workhouse to the excellent homes and schools provided for such children at Padgate. (2) For a long period also prior to the receipt of such circular out-relief to aged deserving persons had been applied upon an improved scale to that obcaining in former years. Each case was dealt with on the merits and in the judgment of the Guardians the relief granted was adequate. (3) Far a period antecedent to such circular, rules were made by the Guardians for distinction of treatment in respect of the aged deserving inmates of the Workhouse and such rules had since obtained. DR. BRAIDE AND HIS HOURS OF ATTENDANCE. APPEAL TO THE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD. The CLERK stated that he had received the following letter from Dr Bra ide, medical officer to the Workhouse, ia reply to a communication which had been sent to him from the Board desiring that he should attend at the Workhouse at eleven o,clock each morning :—" Dear Sir,-1 herewith acknowledge the receipt of your letter of January 12th, relating to a minute of the House Com- mittee, dated December 28th, 1900. As I have placed the matter in the hands of the Local Government Board, I shall forward a copy of your letter to them' and leave the whole case for them to deal with.—Yourstruly,Gao. BRAIDE. To James C Sutton, Esq., clerk." I The CHAIELM&amp;N : Has the doctor been here before eleven o'clock in the morning? Mr WARD: Generally; not always; not every day. The CHAIRMAN: But generally? Mr WAND : Generally, The CHAIRMAN : The Guardians have given an order, and I :should Zink they will insist on it being obeyed- (Hear, hear.) Mr Hicsmax : Aye. THE BOARD SITS PRIVATELY. THE SALARIES QUESTION.</p>
        <p>... The Csarrintsrl said there were several matters on the agenda which it was desirable, he thought, should be considered by the Board, sitting privately. He Would move that the Board, sitting privately, deal with any matters on the agenda, and any other matters which may arise. Dr. PATTERSON seconded,!and the motion was unani- mously agreed to. The reporters and officials then left the Board Room. From information supplied by the Clerk afterwards, we understand that it was decided to free Mr. Shearer from his duties as school attendance officer, and that the sum of £25 per annum which he received those duties be added to his salary as assis- tant relieving officer. Mr Wright is to be relieved of his duties as school attendance officer in the Poulton district and allocated to the Great Sankey district, where he will be responsible for the school attendance duties recently in charge of Mr Shearer. Mr Aukland will succeed Mr Wright as School Attendance Officer in the Poulton district at a salary of £25 per annum. It was also decided to increase Mr Garnett's salary as relieving officer by £lO per annum. The appointments relating to the School Attendance Officers will come into force at the close of the financial year. APPOINTMENT OF A NIGHT PORTER. The Board decided to appoint Mr John L Porter as night porter, at a salary of £25 per annum, with board and lodging, subject to the usual deductions under the Poor Law Superannuation Act, 1896.</p>
        <p>DELICEOUS MAZAWATTEE TEA DELICIOUS MAZAWATTER TEA. DELICIOUS MAZAWATTEB TEA SOLE AGENTS; HENRY MILLING ADD CO„ LTD. WHOLESALE AND RETAIL GROCERS ETO., S. HORSEHAREET.STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 18, 1901</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 18, 1901</p>
        <p>OAKLAND-STRERT WESLEYAN P.S.E., WIDNES. A very pleasing concert was given on Saturday evening, under the presidency of the Rev J Pedlar " The Two Cities," and " Eternal Rest " received excellent treatment at the hands of Mrs. Jenkins. Master W Lees receited well " Sergeant, call the roll," and" Dying for his children." The whistling solos of Mr ,N Smith gave universal satisfaction, " The song that reached my heart "being capitally rendered. The old favourite songs, entitled, " Ora pro nobis," and.' On the Banks of Allan Water," were exquisitely contributed by Messrs Roberts, Thomas, Baggeridge, and Wilkinson Miss Harrison enhanced her reputation with "The Angel of Love," while "The Children's Home and "Lastnight," by Mr ER Roberts,were beautifully interpreted." There is a land," by Mr W R Thomas, created quite an impression; while in company with Miss Thomas ' he rendered with taste and feeling, " Love Divine." "The Children of a King" was feelingly sung by Miss Thomas. Mr W Bartrop accompanied with his usual ability. ENTERTAINMENT AT DUTTON WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>On Friday the Ladies' Vi3iting Committee gave ' their annual tea and entertainment to the inmates of this house. The inmates sat down to tea, which consisted of pork pies, seed cake, &amp;c., at 4 p.m. in the dining hall, which was very prettily decorated, and one and all enjoyed the good things provided. After tea the lathes present consisting of Miss Dutton, Mrs Milner and party, Mrs Baxter and Mrs Whitley and party invited the Guardians present and the visitors, Master and Matron and the other officials of the house to tea, during which time the dining hall was cleared and made ready for the entertainment, which commenced by the Chairman, Mr Stokes, giving an excellent address, eulogising the good work done by the Ladies Visiting Com- mittee and thanking them in the names of the , Guardians of the Union for their kindness to the inmates in giving them such a nice treat. The children under the Master accompanied by Miss Cannah, sang several carols. A. pianoforte and violin duet by Miss Whitley and Miss Bell and comic songs by Mr Youd were received by hearty applause. The gramaphone was exhibited by Mr. Atherton,and pleased the children famously. The girls recited several pieces very creditably, one little girl especially distinguishing herself. The boys went through physical exercises, Indian club drill, and dumbbell exercise. Mr. Baxter then pro- posed a vote of thanks to the Ladies' Visiting Com- mittee and he congratulated the Master and Matron 1 on the splendid condition of the House the cleanli- ness and orderliness therein, showing great care and attention on their part, and drew the attention of the visitors to the report of Mr. Dansey the inspector, who recently visited the House, and in which he reported so favourably; Mr Clarke and Mrs Spruce supported Mr Baxter, and the enter- tainment was drawn to a close by the singing of the National Anthem.—At the meeting of the Guardians on the motion of Mr ttokes a very hearty vote of thanks was accorded the ladies.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[By a Correspondent.]</p>
        <p>There seems to be great trouble at Weston Point as to how the inhabitants of that important and growing place should dispose of their ash-pit refuse. Inspector Farrington, of the Runcorn Union, in- formed the District Council at their meeting on Tuesday that great difficulty was being experienced in the matter, and later a small Committee was suggested and appointei to "see into it." Now, small Committees are all very well, but it savours too mush of doing the Inspector's work. The same practice was *resorted to in the Thelwall business, a small com- mittee was appointed to " see into it," and the responsibility of the Inspector and Medical Officer of health in a great measure, thus became covered. The result has been the interference of the Local ' Government Board. One would think the Council's best course would be to get their Inspector and Surveyors to " see into the matter," and prepare something for its remedy ; and by keeping them- selves on the outside, the members of the Council can, the better, bring their capabilities to bear upon the ultimate decision. This course seems to be the better one, as no one would question the superior technical knowledge of the Inspector and Survey- ors in devising a remedial scheme.</p>
        <p>The Transporter Bridge, it will be noticed is the subject of a special supplement given with this issue. It may be interesting to know that the whole of the shares have been allotted, that Widnes has taken £25,000; Runcorn £lO,OOO and Sir J T Brunner,,Bart, M.P., £20,000. Thus Runcorn can feel assured of a capable representation, as Sir John has always given to the town the benefit of his hearty support. The share capital amounts to £lOO,OOO, Building operations will be commenced before the century is very much older, and Runcorn is yet destined to be the home of the first English "ferry in the air."</p>
        <p>The town was alive onThursday with rumours to the effect that theßuncorn police had captured the " Liverpool murderer," but inquiries in the proper quarters soon eligited the fact that rumour was but rumour. It is not beyond the possibility, however, for the wanted man to have taken shelter in Run- corn, and the townspeople would be well advised to consult the police quietly about any suspicious characters enquiring for lodgings. Honest travel- lers will not resent the police; and it will not matter much if rogues do,provided ultimately they get their desserts.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL. Millom were defeated as expected. There are few games that have taken place this year on the Irwell-lane ground, that have been as interesting as last Saturday's fixture. A contemporary dubbed it "A Jolley game" inasmuch as the redoubtable half- back (" Jimmy ") scored 16 points out of the total (22), and landed five goals out of six shots at the uprights. Yet that was not the only feature of the game, by a long way, it was full of features: Houghton letting two tries pass him was one thing; and, Sam Walker gaining another, which should have been no try at all ; but, perhaps Sammy dessrves one, for there is no doubt he has been done out of tries innumerable. Still, it would be more satisfactory all round, if people get what they deserve when they deserve it; and we bad les of this compensating by error system. On Saturday Runcorn play Leigh, at Leigh, and ought to win. They leave with a strong team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>LAUNCH OF THE ".TROUT:"</p>
        <p>We learn that the Runcorn Anglers' Association have just launched a comfortably equipped fishing boat, "the Trout," on their exclusive waters at the "Old Quay." The function was one in which several of the most enthusiastic members took a great interest. A CORRECTION. .</p>
        <p>We regret, having recorded, in Our report of the St Paul's Ladies' Bible Class, the words " through death," used in referring to what should have bsen "the loss sustained to the class through Mrs Soulby's • removal." ' The error was:committed by inference, from the tone in which the information was given our reporter, and not intentionally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>11014 LE MISSIONS AT ST. P LUTA CHORCO..</head>
        <p>11014 LE MISSIONS AT ST. P LUTA CHORCO..</p>
        <p>The annual sermons on behalf of the Wesleyan Home Mission will be preached at St Paul's Church Runcorn on Sunday next, by the Rev fl Howard May. doming at 10-30, and evening at 6 o'clock. The annual meeting will follow at the same place, on the Monday, when Mr J R Frith will take the chair at seven o'clock. A hearty invitation is ex- tended to all, and being the first effort in the New Century it is hoped the collections will establish a record. See advt. ANNUAL PRESENTATION OF BIBLES AT HALTON. The scholars at the Halton Trinity Wesleyan Church, were on Sunday last presented with beauti- fully bound gilt-edged Bibles (revised version), that is, those of them]who merited the gifts as a reward for their diligence and regular attendance. The presentation was fittingly carried out by Mr Ast- bury (senior superintendent). Addresses were delivered by Messrs J B Clarke and James Looker, and also by the Rev A Wells (superintendent Run- corn Circuit.) The number of successful scholars was 22. "THE LIVERPOOL MURDERER."</p>
        <p>On Thursday, before Mr. It Imison, Henry Han- kinson, an old offender, was remanded to the fol- lowing day, on a charge of loitering with intent to commit a felony in the vicinity of Runcorn Docks, —ln connection with the arrest of the prisoner, rumours were afloat throughout the town that the Liverpool murderer had been secured and it was not until late in the day that the sensation caused thereby abated,—Peter Carter, of Runcorn, was dis- charged. The charge preferred against him was sleeping out. RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. The magistrates (Messrs H Reynolds and W Handley) had little to do at the above Sessions, last Monday.—James Clarke, a ten year old boy, sum- moned, at the instance of his fcher, for stealing a shirt, valued 3s, the father's property, was on the calandar:; but the Bench advised the father to with- draw it, and administer correction himself. They felt it would be a pity to let a conviction of that nature hang over the lad all his life.—Thomas Helsby" a soldier of the Queen" and a native of Run- corn, was fined 20s for disobedience of summons, charging him with using obscene language in Loch- street,on the 16th July 1900. It appeared begot the summons and left the town. Subsequently he en- listed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>THEFT OF A TABLECLOTH.</head>
        <p>THEFT OF A TABLECLOTH.</p>
        <p>A middle-aged woman, named Margaret Fenny, 104, Farnworth-street, was charged at Widnes on Monday, with stealing a tablecloth, value 2s, the property of William Wylie, 12, Farnworth-street, on the sth instant—Superintendent Keighley stated that Mrs R ylie was not able to be present owing to illness, but on the sth of this month Mrs Wylie went into the backyard and left the tablecloth on the table. When she returned the cloth was gone. She suspected prisoner and charged her with the theft, but she denied it, but afterwards acknow- ledged that she had sold it for 33 to a Mrs Mulroy. As prisoner pleaded guilty, there was no need for Mrs Wylie to appear as a witness.—Prisoner was fined 10s and costs or 14 days,—The fine was paid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MERSEY MISSIO:N 'TO SEAMEN%</head>
        <p>MERSEY MISSIO:N 'TO SEAMEN%</p>
        <p>The chief attraction here, last Tuesday night,was the living skeleton, and large indeed was the aud- ience that flocked to see one of Barnum's late at- tractions, who had kindly consented to exhibit himself for the benefit of the mission. The concert party was provided by Mr. Byfield, and the presi- dent for the evening was Mr. J K Diggas, Mr. E Roberts was deservedly encored for " Killarney," Mr George Taylor was also well received in " Mar- gerite" and "Sweet Marie," Miss L Gibson favoured with a delightful song, and Miss Lily Shaw, one of the Mission favourites sang with her usual effect "Have you see my dolly." Captain Tucker gave several seafaring songs, and altogether a delightful evening was spent. Miss Rodd and Mr W Grounds acted as accompanists. PROMOTION FOR A RUNCORN CUSTOMS OFFICER. We are pleased to announce the promotion of Mr Horace Rodd, who has been second officer in charge at the Port of Runcorn, for the past two years. During the time Mr Rodd has been in the town he has made numerous friends. He identified himself also very closely with the Mersey ML9sion to sea- men, and his removal will not only be a loss to Mr Shaw., the missioner, but also to the many workers connected with the place. Mr Rodd was it will be remembered the chairman of the last bazaar, and worked .arduously for the raising of funds for the painting and re-decoration of the Mission Mise Rodd, his daughter.has also identified her- self with the weekly concerts to the sail- ors as accompanist, and her gservice3 will be greatly missed. Mr Rodd leaves Runcorn on Mon- day to take up his duties at Goole, and we wish that he and his family may live and enjoy his well- merited promotion. ST. PAUL'S P.S E., WIDNES. On Saturday, at the Parochial Rooms, Mr W Laws presided over a very large company. In "The song that reached my heart" and "Sweet Genevieve" Miss M Molvneux proved herself to be the possessor of a very flexible voice. Mr Thomp- son's " Two Spoons" was a very humorous contribu- tion and quite tickled the audience, while " The Sunny South "by Mr Rawlins was equally enjoy- able. The violin solos and duets by Miss C Mather and Mr C T McCririe were executed with skill and ability, the" Bohemian Girl " by the latter artiste being exceptionally well rendered. Miss Houghton's and Miss Caton's pianoforte solos were highly appreciated while the piano trio by Misses Caldwell and Donaldson,and Mr Rawlins gave every satisfac- tion Mrs Martin and Miss Mathers contributed to the success of the meeting by their performing the duties of accompanists in a highly creditable manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WEEK OF SPECIAL PRAYER.</head>
        <p>A WEEK OF SPECIAL PRAYER.</p>
        <p>The worshippers at St. Paul's Church, Runcorn, have this week been engaged in special prayer, and the gatherings, each day, have been helpful and beneficial. In the afternoons the meetings have been held in the minister's vestry, and in the even- ing in the morning chapel. The course for the week has been: Short Bible reading each day as follows :—Monday, Rev Alfred Wells ; Tuesday,Mrs Robinson; Wednesday, Mrs Morris; Thursday, Mrs Wells, and Friday, Mrs Simkin. The evening meet- ings have been addressed as follows :—Monday "The Church and the Home," Mr Coventry; Tues- day, "The Church and the Prayer meeting," Rev J B Foster; Wednesday, "The Church and the Class Meeting," Mr T Simister ; Thursday, " The Church and the Young," Rev Alfred Wells; Friday, "The Church and the Masses," Mr R El Posnete; and on Saturday, "Power from on High," will 6e the subject of the Rev H Morrison's discourse, Hymns for Mission Services have been sung at each gathering._</p>
        <p>TEA. PARTY AT VICTORIA-ROAD SCHOOLS, RUNCORN. In connection with the continuatign classes at the Victoria-road Board Schools, a tea party and entertainment was given oil Friday evening by the members of the cooking class—who are taught by Miss Polly Simkin—to the members of the sewing class, at the head of which are the Misses Shaw and Wood. The room had seen specially laid out for the occasion, and the table was also prettil3 decor- ated. At least 84 enjoyed an excellent repast, and after the room had been partially cleared games were indulged in. A very pleasing programme was also submitted, which made the proceedings addi- tionally enjoyable. A fitting prelude to the subse- quent items was a pianoforte duet entitled" Home sweet home," the artistes, Miss Catherine Shall- cross and ies L Whitfield, displaying a very good acquaintance with the instrument. Miss Hilda Williams recited very pathetically "Billy's dead and gone to glory." "Watching for Pa" was prettily sung by Miss Eva Gayter, and that old favourite song " The old f elks at home " was very tunefully rendered by Miss Clara Leins. Miss Ellen Summer- field added considerahly to the success of the even- ing by a brilliantly rendered violin solo. An appropriate recitation by Miss Eva Limdops con- cluded a most enjoyable evening, and everyone went home thoroughly pleased with the party. DELICIOUS MAZAWATTRE IBA DBLICIOUS MAZAWATTIIII TEA DELICIOUS MAZAWATTBB TEA SOLE AGENTS: HENRY MILLING+ A Co., LTD. WHOLESALE AND RETAIL GROCERS ETC., 5, HORSEHAREET-STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>DEATH OF THE VICAR OF DARESBURY.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE VICAR OF DARESBURY.</p>
        <p>The news will be received with regret of the death of the Rev. Henry Saprell Clark, M.A., Vicar of All Saint's, Daresbury, which took place at his residence at 4.15 on Wednesday afternoon. The rev. gentleman had been confined to his house for the past eight weeks, and was attended by several medical men, but despite their unremitting attention, he passed away at the time stated. Peritonitis was the cause of death. The deceased was appointed Vicar of Daresbury in 1896, on the resignation of the Rev Thomas Whitley, whose daughter he had married. He was held in high respect by the parishioners and his demise will occasion much regret. The deceased clergyman was 45 years of age. He was chairman of Daresbury School Board, land took an interest in several local institutions, including the Moore and District Horticultural Society. He leaves a widow, but no children.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT - COUNCIL. Mr J Knowles, J.P., presided over the usual fort- nightly meeting of this Council, held at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday. There were also present : Admiral J.P. Jones-Parry, Messrs S Woodward, T Baxter, CE Linaker, J B Chadwick, T Wright, J Stokes, J Mclntyre, T Dorlington, J Baugley, T Lewis, P Gleave W Fletcher, G Pilling, E Clarke, P Leather, T Antvviss, E A Wallington, with Messrs George F Ashton (clerk), W Diggle, and J Swinton, (surveyors), J Farrington (inspector), and Dr Adams, (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>HATTON SEWAGE SCHEHE.</head>
        <p>THE HATTON SEWAGE SCHEHE.</p>
        <p>Mr Swinton reported that the committee appointed to consider the Hatton Sewage echeme he had prepared, recommended its rejection on do grounds that its esti- mated initial cost, £450 was too great. Mr T Baxter said that they had only a moment or two before the Council meeting, to consider the plans, but they thought the scheme was too elaborate and too costly for Hatton and that some simpler and cheaper scheme should be obtained or it would be ruinous for the Hatton ratepayers. Mr Woodward considered that to involve Hatton rate- payers in £450 of an initial expense for a sewerage scheme would be a most unretsonable thing, and be thought it was the duty of the Council to impress up3n their surveyor the desirability of preparing a simple scheme of settling tanks and filtration so as to bring the actual cost to as low a figure as possible in the interests of the ratepayers. He considered that £2OO would be ample. The Clerk added that the Vi'arrington Corporation might force their hands very shortly if something was not done. Mr. Swinton then stated that be had considered the scheme he suggested would nave been cheap, but on going into it he found it was not so. Still he thought he would be able to devise something suitable.</p>
        <p>- - CARBOLIC &amp; POISON. —BBB' REGULATIONS. Mr S B Provis, secretary, Local Government Board, forwarded copy of an order made by Her Majesty's Most Honourable Privy Council approving a resolu- tion passed by the Pharmaceutical Society of Great Britain that liquid preparations of carbolic acid and its homologues containing more than three per cent of those substances should, except in certain cases can - fleeted with agriculture and horticulture be deemed poisons within the meaning of the Pharmacy Act 1868, and the second part of Schedule A to that Act. The Board had reason to believe that, in a very large num- ber of cases where local authorities disinfect or pro- cure the disinfection of premises, and things which have been exposed to infection, the dis- iafectant employed is carbolic acid. They desire to take this opportunity, therefore, of pointing out that whenever the disinfectant employed is carbolic acid or any other poison within the meaning of the Pharmacy Act 1868, only bottles similar to those prescribed by the regulations adopted by the Pharmaceutical Society of Great Britain and approved by an Order of the Lords of the Council, dated 31st January, 1899, should be used to contain it. A copy of the last mentioned Order was also enclosed. The Clerk intimated he would forward a copy of the Order to the Inspector.</p>
        <p>TROUBLE AT EMSTON POINT. In his report Mr. Farrington referred to the fact that there was a great difficulty at Weston Point in disposingyf naidden ref 090.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chadwick concurred. Mr. Linaker : Cannot you put it in the Ship Canal ? (Laughter.). .</p>
        <p>'The Chiirman : Yes, it's tidal there, (Increased laughter.) _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Stokes : Why don't the farmers take it Mr. Chadwick : There are only two farmers in Wes- ton Point, and they have their hand full of cinders and to_such an extent.-...</p>
        <p>----.— The Inspector: It was a question of pots, pans and old iron, not cinders alone. Mr. Stokes: I thought so. That was why I asked the question. Such stuff ruins land especially potato ground. Dr Adams thought that the question could be righted by a general adoption of the" pail system," and the use of a destructor. Ultimately, on the motion of Mr S Woodward, second- ed by Mr J B Chadwick, it was decided, to relegate the matter to the following committee :--Aleears J Stokes, J B Chadwick,T Wright, and James Tudor. BLINDNESS. EYESIGHT EBSTORED.--DISEASES OF THE BYE CURED without operation. Book with full par- ticulars, free bypost on mentioning this paper.— Address W. MELLOR, Eye Specialist. 236,0af0rd road, Manhester. -</p>
        <p>C RAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS, CRISP AND CREAMY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>--..,w--. NORTFIWICH PARLIAMENTARY DIVISION.</head>
        <p>--..,w--. NORTFIWICH PARLIAMENTARY DIVISION.</p>
        <p>The electoral register is generally considered to be a fairly reliable record of the progress or decay of a locality and viewed from this standpoint, the Parliamentary register of the Northwich Division for 1901, which has just been issued, presents some interesting features. The constituency, though con- taining a !considerable agricultural element, may be described generally as a manufacturing one, in- cluding important carrying trades by its rivers and canals. In the agricultural district of Aston-by- :Sutton and Weaverham there is a decrease of 9 and 11 respectively,while Davenb am remains stationary The Northwich district, while retaining suf- ficient of its ancient industry to be still entitled to its name of " salt town " and the bead-quarters of the Weaver Navigation is still more widely known as the home of the chemi- cal manufacturers of Messrs Branner Mond &amp; Co., which have a world-wide reputation. Here also there is a decrease to record. It is however of a very slight nature. In me three wards into which the town is divided, there is a net falling off of 10. Until recent years Middlewich was a quaint old- world "sleepy hollow" devoted to agriculture and salt production; it is now rising into importance as a chemical centre. At the same time the salt trade is extending. Another feature of great trade interest is the meeting here of the North Stafford- shire and Shropshire Union Canals. Middlewich is growing. The increase in voters is 20 equal to an increased population of about six times that number. Winsford with salt manufacturing as its staple industry and as the headquarters of the Salt Union may be considered as fast displacing Northwich as the Cheshire salt capital ; and being situate at the navigable head of the river Weaver necessarily has the large water traffic which is carried on by a fleet of steam-barges Iconveying the brine-manufactured salt to Weston , Point, Runcorn and Liverpool for shipment. The increase however, is very slight. Wharton Ward 1 has decreased 20 voters, while Over Ward has in- creased four over that number, so that the chief feature appears to be a transference of population from one part of the locality to another. Considerable progress has been made in Runcorn and Weston where the chemical trade is brighter and the shipping trade is extending, especially in the importation of timber and wheat to the lay by. There is a very distinct increase in the electorate. Runcorn North has advanced by 73 voters and Runcorn South by 21 voters, a total increase of 94 in Runcorn. At Weston the increase is the very substantial one of 23 veters. Even Halton, dependent upon agricul- ture and tanning is moving, if only slowly, the electorate being increased by 4. The net increase in the entire constituency is 103. Appended is an interesting tabulated comparative statement : Polling district. 19)1. 1900. Inc. dec. Asto..-by-Sutton Barnton Davenham Halton Marston 428 ... 429 ... 1 Middlewich 954 .. 934 20 ... Northwich (Castle) Northwich (Let twich) 541 ... 654 ... ... 13 Northwich (Witton) 1571 ... 1587 ... .„ 16 Runcorn North 2045 ... 1972 ... 73 ... Runcorn South . 963 ... 939 ... 21 Weaverhara 446 456 ... 11</p>
        <p>Weston - Wilmington - Winsford (Cover) - Wineftml(VVharton) 686 .. 606 .. -- .. 20</p>
        <p>Totals... 12176 12367 186</p>
