      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday. Mr J Moseley- Williams presided, at.J there were presew :—Messrs A R Norman, S Taylor, Charles Reynolds, the Rural Dis- trict Councillors, with Mr George F Ashton (clerk), and Mr W E Hough (assistant clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MASTER'S HEALTH.</head>
        <p>THE MASTER'S HEALTH.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the Master was ill,and unable to be present. The cook wished to leave that day, and the matron recommended that Alice Brow tibia be ap- pointed at £22 per annum. It appeared that the Visit- ing committee had approved of the recommendation.— On the motion of Mr A R Norman, the report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>'ABSENCE OF THE WORKHOUSE MEDICAL OFFICER.</head>
        <p>'ABSENCE OF THE WORKHOUSE MEDICAL OFFICER.</p>
        <p>Dr. Selby wrote as follows :—Owing to the illness of my mother I may be absent from two or three of my ordinary visits to the Workhouse next week, if so, Dr. Dutton, my locum tenons will visit whilst lam North. Trusting this will meea with the Guardians approval.— Writing at a later stage he said,"l have just seen a foot note in my report book to-day asking me for a report and to attend next committee meeting. I have made all arrangements for going North to-morrow (January 11th) for the reasons I have already informed you and I trust I am not asking too much for being allowed to defer my report &amp;c, until next meeting of the Board."</p>
        <p>'The Chairman thought unjet the circumstances the doctor was to be excused.</p>
        <p>VACCINATION FEES. Mr. Linaker asked whether the vaccination fees were not to be considered again ? The Chairman: The idea was that they should run for twelve months and then be revised.</p>
        <p>Mr Linaker : When will the time expire ? The Chairman: j think in April.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker : The vaccination fees are very high,and if anything could be done to get them reduced it should be done. (Hear, hear.) The Chairman : Yes. I think that is the general idea.</p>
        <p>The matter then dropped. FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Out-relief was granted to Runcorn £B5 ; Frodsbam, £23 ' • Budwortb, £2B. The balance last Board day was £920 5s 2,1 ; received since, £lB6 76 53 ; cheques required, £877 93 8J ; leaving a balance of £429 33 lid.. The financial report was adopted on the motion of Mr Woodward, seconded by Mr T Wright.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>RUNCORN SAVINGS BANK. Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SAVINGS BANK. Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>A Prosperous Year Concluded.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the managers and trus- tees of the Runcorn Savings Bank, took place at the Bank on Thursday afternoon. Mr E Imison pre- sided, and there were also present : —Messrs J LI Frith, J M Frith, J Littler, J Pennington, J Pritchard, T Jones, W Hankinson, W Fisher, T Wright, with Messrs W Handley (actuary) and C Handley (assistant actuary).</p>
        <p>THE ACTUARY'S REPORT. , Mr. Handley presented We report as follows :—Tbe statement of accounts tor the year ended November 20th, 1900, shows a continuance of the steady progress which has marked the Bank business for a considerable number of years past. During the year, the number of transactions, the number of open accounts, the cash turnover, the stock purchases, and the amount of interest and stock cividends paid and credited to deposi- tors, all show a satisfactory increase. The amount of business has been the largest of any year since the opening of the Bank now nearly 59 years ago. At the commencement of the year the depositors stood thus : November 20th, 1899 : personal depositors 1,109, amount £32.121 ls 9d; charit- able societies 23, amount £1,387 Os 7d ; friendly and sick societiee 81, £9,575 18s 4d. November 20th 1900 : Personal depositors 1,132, amount £33,264 4s 10d ; charitable societies 24, £760 2s ; friendly and sick socie- ties 76, £9,775 13s. A total number of open accounts of 1,232 as against 1,213, and in amount of £43,799 19s lid as compared with £13,054 03 Bd, an increase of 19 and £715 193 3d, respectively. The sum received as deposits and transfers from other Banks has been £13,256 Sc lid in 2,667 transactions, and the amount paid in withdrawals and transfers to other Banks £12,711 15s 4d in 1247 transactions. Consol stock of the nominal value of £1,200 has been purchased for 11 depositors and £250 stock sold for a depositor. Including tise dividends on stock held for depositors, the sum of £1,104 Os lid has been paid and credited to depositors accounts as interest for the year. The amount withdrawn during the year has shown an increase,but this is mainly attributable to a larger range of more remunerative investments being obtainable consequent on the improved trade prospects of tne district. To meet the liabilities of the Bank there was invested on November 20:h 1900 with (National Debt Commissioners as per their certificate) £43,374 12s Id ; and at Parr's Bank, Limited, as local treasurer' and certified by the manager, T C Haddock Eq., £567 8s 21 ;;these amounts showing a surplus of £142 Os 4d over and above the amount ow- ing to depositors' and an increase in the surplus for the year of £2l 193 3d. Of the 1132 personal depositors' accounts, the amount at credit of 606 or more than one half, do net exceed the sum of £lO each. The auditors have with constant regularity attended at the Bank .both during the hours in which it has been open for business, and at other times. They have tested every transaction, and compared a large number of depositors books with the ledgers,and by their certificate stated that they have found all cor- rect. The Inspector under the Savings Bank Acts has made two visits during the year, extending over three days, and has subjected all the books and accounts to a thorough examination.—During the year Mr James Handley, J.P., who as auditor, manager and trustee, served the bank for many years,, and also Mr William Littler, an esteemed manager for a long period, and an honoured representative of the friendly societies of the town and district have been removed by death. With the improving outlook in the business prospects of the town and district, it seems not unreasonable to hope that the close of the year may witness a continuance of steady progress, and add a further testimony to its use- fulness. The Chairman in proposing the adoption of the re, port said their thoughts were directed in the first instance to that portion of the report which referred te the removal by death of Mr James Handley and Mr William Littler. As they were well aware Mr William Littler represented the Friendly Societies and bad done so for a considerable time, and he must say he had been very muca struck with the way he attended the bank and represented those interests he was most particularly associ- ated with The keynote of Mr. Handley's charac- ter was thoroughness. Whatever he undertook he accomplished capably and thoroughly. He gave if anything more personal attention to public matters,than was usually given; and a special feature was the amount of trouble he would go to over public matters. He per- sonally bad never appealed to Mr. Handley on behalf of anything connected with the town, but it always met with a ready and generous reply. He thought the institution had suffered a great loss by the death ,of those gentlemen. With regard to the financial side of the report it was very satisfactory. The table.giving the comparison with the business of the last ten years showed that in that time they had an increase of £14,500. This showed that the Bank occupied a position which was extremely useful, and also that it had the confidence of the people who deposited money there. Having referred to the great safeguards taken by the Inspection Committee under the Savings Bank Acts for assuring accuracy of accounts, the Chairman went on to show that notwith- standing the fact that large amounts bad been with- drawn for investment where a better rate of percentage could be obtained,and that there was a decrease of some £620 in the Charitable Societies, still the business of the past year had shown an increase. He heartily endorsed the last clause of the Actuary's report. The Chairman then formally proposed the adoption of the report and that the Auditors be allowed £4 instead of £3 as hereto- fore. .</p>
        <p>Mr J It Frith seconded and in doing so he regretted he had to deplore the loss of Mr James Handley. He was a gentleman whose grasp of tgures was beyond the ordinary, whose kindness of heart everybody knew; and everything be did, he did with a thoroughness and earnestness that felt it must be done properly. He (Mr Frith) thought he had no need to make any remarks about the progress of the Beak. But he would say that he considered the success of the Bank to be in a great measure due to Mr W Handley. The report was adopted unanimously and the usual number ordered to be printed and circulated.</p>
        <p>OMER BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>The certificate of the Auditors and the certificate of the National Debt Commissioners were then produced and examined. _ .</p>
        <p>A vote of condolence recording the loss the institution had suffered by the deaths of Mr. Handley and Ms. Littler was then recorded in the minutes.</p>
        <p>The Cnairman,iiTii-ii,;;ifi:iiin-T3i Pritchard, Mr J R Frith and the Actuary were appointed as a com- mittee to consider the revision of the rules of the Institution. Votes of thanks to the Chairman and Actuary con- cluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>LADIES' BEST GLACE Beots AND SH UNDEE THE CLOCK, BRIDE-STREET.—Advt. TO CURE A COLD IN ONE DAY . .</p>
