      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LECTURE AT WARRINGTON BY PROFESSOR STIRLING. Air and Water.</head>
        <p>LECTURE AT WARRINGTON BY PROFESSOR STIRLING. Air and Water.</p>
        <p>Professor Stirling, M.D., Professor of Physiology at Owens College, continued his course of lectures on "Food and Nutrition," under tha auspices of the Combo Trust, in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street, Warrington, on Tuesday evening. The hall was well filled. At tne outset, the Lecturer briefly reviewed the principal points of the preceding lecture on the wear and tear of the body. Food was necessary in order to supply matter to repair the daily loss of the body and for the growth and repair of all growing organisms, and also to supply the energy we needed to do our work. It was plain that in order to maintain the continual loss which went on there must be a steady supply of food. Reference was made to the varying appetites of people at different ages. There was no chemical element in their bodies which was not present in inorganic nature. Dealing with the composition of food, be said there were many varieties but many of them required to be cooked or digested before they could be made use of by the tissues of the body. All foods, whether animal, vegetable, or mineral, belonged to a few .roups. These might be classified into six (1) oxygen, (2) water, (3) mineral matter or inorganic salts, (4) auto-hydrates. (5) fats (8) proteids. Be proposed to speak of two, the air they breathed and the water they occasionally drank. (Laughter).</p>
        <p>Atmosphere was a comparatively simple affair. ItS constitution was practically the same all over the globe. It was composed principally of oxygen and nitrogen. Nitrogen was by far the more abundant. Oxygen was the essential portion for them. It was taken into the blood, and by that means carried to the diff2rent parts of the body. By the circulation of the blood it was brought within the reach of every living cell and every [ living element of the constitution. It was a substance of supreme importance. But for its presence there could be no possibility for vegetable or animal on the globe. Its importance could be gauged by the fact that front 1,500 to 2,000 gallons, passed through one person's lungs every 24 hours. Air was the most important of our foods although we did not recognise it as food. He spoke of the counteracting effects of nitrogen, of which there is four times as much in the air as oxygen, and passed on to speak of germs or microbes and their action for good or evil. The air was loaded w:ttiparticles of dust, some of it being "dead dust", and some of it "live dust." He ea. plained how extremely microscopic the microbes are and described their appearance. Although small, he observed, they Lad enormous powers of reproduction. There was no other living thing which reproduced itself with such rapidity. The popular idea of germs centred round pestilential ones. Those, however, were the black sheep among a great number.</p>
        <p>I Microbes were plants. It took a long time to discover that, and a still longer that as other plants they could divide or produes little seeds. Tne discoveries of Francesco Redi, of Florence, and the researches of Spallanzani and Pasteur in this direction were alluded to. Pasteur proved to the whole world that there was no such thing as spontaneous generation. The knowledge which was handed down had resulted in the discoveries of Lister, and the adoption throughout the world of antiseptic surgery, by which wounds could be so treated as to prevent putrefaction. The Lecturer proceeded to explain that some of tna germs were great scavengers, and referred to the b3autiful organic balance in nature. The use of plants in absorbing the carbonic acid from the impurified air was mentioned in this con- nection. I:loafing with the means for killing the noxious microbes, the Lecturer ref :3rred to the efficacy of fire land boiling water. Sunshine was also one of the most Ipowerful instruments in this connection. Alluding to water, the lecturer said it was difficult to snake people realise tbe importance of it both from the individual and public point of view. Water was the dominant part of every person, About two-thirds of an individual was composed of water. The tissues could only live in running water. The importance of the purity of water was a point he need not insist upon. When Glasgow was visited by the cholera epidemic in 1832, the water then being supplied from the Clyde, there were 2,800 deaths in what was then a comparatively small town. The source of supply being the same in 1851, the deaths numbored 5,900. Later when the great wave of cholera rolled over the land, the city having ob- tained, a pure water supply from Lake Katrine, the deaths were only 63, and the city had of course by then grown enormously. A similar tale could be told of London, while they were all familiar with the cause of the dread enteric which had taken the lives of so many of their soldiers in South Africa, Pure water was Mose. acutely necessary for healthy life and this fact was re. I ognised by the early Romans, who tonk great pains to get a large and wholesome supply of water Tne dan- gers of shallow wells was incidentally in-ntioned. In referring to artificial and mineral waters, the work of Dr. Priestley,who conducted many of his experiments at Warrington, was sympathetically alluded to Reference was also made to the iroc,rganic salts in the body, and to the absolute necessity of salt for existence. Numerous experiments illustrating the subject were made,and a number of slides also assisted in the elucida- tion of the lecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
