      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>" HOME " AGAIN.</head>
        <p>" HOME " AGAIN.</p>
        <p>The charms of " Home " ga with us, wherever duty or pleasure calls, whether in peaceful occupations, or in the stern necessities of war. How intensely precious must " Home" be to those who are retnrning from the struggles and hardships endured in South Africa. Think of the sick and wounded invalided" Home," to the care of mother, wife, or friends, who are only too anxious to have their dear ones with them azain, to nurse them back into a new life of perfect health and strength. At such a time, the great recuperative Tonic is Gwily m Evans' Quinine Bitters. It gives power to the weak limbs, it steadies the nerves, brightens the eye, infuses new hope, and builds up the system anew. bilany I patients that have been suffering from Enteric and other wasting diseases, simply require to be systematically treated with a tonic restorative. A complete rest and a 'reviving tonic, is all they require. In Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, this will be found in its highest perfec- tion. The Best Remedy for Loss of Appetite, Indigestion. Impure Blood, Chest Diseases, and Influenza. DON'T LOSE TIME,</p>
        <p>People are continually wondering where to go for some new remedy, when they feel" Out of sorts," " Notup to the mark," or "only Middling." We say "Don't go," come and get the well-tried and proved Remedy Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. Do you suger from Indigestion? Do you feel " Tired "at your work ? If so, take Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters and you will soon bo right again. Chronic sufferers are sometimes averse to be always changing their medicine, and rightly so, but it does happen at times that a change would lead to a better state of thing. For instanue, if you are suffering from extreme weakness, and are gradually making up your mind to a prolonged illness, you are making a great mistake if you have not tried Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. Will you try it ? Don't lose time. It is the best remedy for General D,,bility, Low Spirits. and Nervousness. Its tonic qua:Ries infuse new life, and you soon see the sunny siue of nature again, and cheerful content comes with a gradual gain of strength. For Chest Affections, and to guard off Influenza, try this excellent vegetable tonic restorative. Be sure that you get it, when you ask for it. Don't buy worthless imitations. Say you want Gwilyrn Evans' Quinine Bitters, and see that the name" Gwilym Evans' "is on the label, on the stamp. and on the battle, without which none are genuine. Gwilyni Evans' Quinine Bitters is sold in bottles, 23 93 and 43 63 each, by Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors, or will be sent, carriage free, direct from The Sole Proprietors : Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Cmpany, Limited, LlanellY, South Wales:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATE</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATE</p>
        <p>ATTEMPTED SUICIDE.—A. WAERINGTONIWOMAN TAKES LAUDANUM. Elizabeth Molyneux, who resides at 17, Miller-street, was brought before the Warring;ton magistrates on Fri- day, on a charge of attempting to commit suicide by , taking laudanum on Monday last at her residence.—The Chief Constable stated that at 11.45 on the morning in question, Sergeant Hollingsworth was in the neighbour- hood and be received information which caused him to go to the house of the prisoner, who was the wife of a very respectable locksmith, fie found her lying unconscious from the effects of some I narcotic draught. Dr Kisby was called in and rendered very valuable assistance, and having some patients t visit, left the woman in charge of the police officer, with instructions as to how to continue the treatment. Ul- timately prisoner was removed to the Infirmary where she had remained until that day. Questioned as to what she had taken she said she had taken three pennyworth of laudanum which she purchased at the shop of Mr Thompson, chemist, Knutsford-rosd. She also stated that the reason the committed the rash act was because she had a few words with her husband on Sunday night with respect to some dcmestic matter of a trivial nature. She now seemed very penitent and promised faithfully that she would Dever repeat this foolish and almost fatal act, and with the permission cf the Bench he would withdraw the cbarge.—Tbe magistrates agreed to this course.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>MINOR CASES. -- Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. -- Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>IfAIDAY —Before the Mayor (in the chair). Messrs. Heplderd. It Young and Thornton. WHOLESALE THEFTS OF SWEETS arc. —Fred Roberts. 87, New Crown-street. at. Helens, was charged on a warrant with stealing two jars of mincemest,value 15 10dthe property of William Henry Lawless on the 18th of December.—lie ' pleaded guilty—Mr Browne prosecuted on behalf of Messrs. W U and C Lawless, sugar bodere Market Place and Rose and Crown-street, and stated that cl,fendant was employed as a sugar boiler at the Rose and Crown-street Works. Cafortunately Mr Lawless. Was taken ill In August and was away from the works until December He suspected nothing wrong until two girls, who worked in the same room as the defendant, gave certain information, with the result that inquiries were made and it was discovered that the man had been robbing the firm during the whole time Mr Lawless waa ill. His modus operandi was as follows. The defendant had his meals sent from his lodgings to the works, and instead of sending the basket back empty, he ordered the girls to take sweets &amp;a. trick in it almost every time. In Christmas wee,e he sent two round jars of mincemeat and a large quantity of boiled toffee to a cereain public house. Mr Lawless considered that the man had been robbing him right and left since August.—A fine of 205 and costs with the option of a mc.nth's Imprisonment was Imposed. UNSOUND TURKETs,—John Oxley, 27.4,Enutsford-road, was summoned for exposing for sale two turkeys, which were un- fit foe human food, oa the 4th inst.--s inspector Norman stated that the turkeys in question were exposed on a stall belonging to the defendant, in the yard of the Big Horse Shoe Inn. They were dressed in the usual way for sale. 01, examination ha found them to be in a decomposed state and quite unfit for human food.—Defendant who stated that the turkeys were not intsnded for sale, but for the use of him- self and his son, was lined 201. WIFE DESERTION. —A BRUTAL AMUSEMENT.—JOhII Henry Lee., whose address was given as the '• Red Lion Hotel" was summoned for deserting his wife. BlizabAh Lea, 46, West- street, on the 13th of December —Mr. Browne appeared for the Complainant, and Mr Evans represented the defendant.— The case for the complainant Was that for the last two months defendant had never come home sober. On the night in question she went to bed and left him downstairs and on getting up the next morning found the gas still burn- ing. the front door locked, and the key in the middle of the street. She had never seen the man since, He bad not. given her money for eight weeks.—Complainant gave evi- dence as to the unhappy life which she had been leading and stated that in order to aggravate her, the defendant would set fire to cats' tails, and also threaten to cut their heads off, She kept a small shop, by which she could maintain herself, and she asked for a separation order with an allowance to keep the children.—Defendant stated that on the night named he went to la,d and his wife Came into the bedroom .and told him to clear out of the house, as he was only a lodger. She threatened to stick a knife into him so ha left the house.—The Bench made a separation order with 10s. per week alimony. Mosnay.—Before Mr r Monks (pregiding),Alderrnan Heaton. Councillor Smethurst. Messrs. Birchen, J R Young, S Wright, Hayward, and Thornton. THEFT FROM THE c.t.c.—Willtam Baker, a young man residing at 21, Lloyd..street, was charged on a warrant with stealing a number of iron railway chairs of the value of 6s, the property of the Cheshire Lines Committee. on the 3r..1 of January of last year.—The Chief Constable stated the facts of the case, which were to the effect that near Be wsey siding a pile of iron chairs was stocked. Some information reached a man named Peter Phillips. a foreman platelayer in the ser- vice of the committee in this district, anal he visited the 'premises of a man named Wellings, who had rented a piece of land near Froghall Bridge, and under a sack. In a state of semi-concealment, be found a number of stolen railway chairs That led to the arrest and prosecution of Wellings, who was fined. The present prisoner's name cropped up. Welllngs alleging that be received the property from him, not know- ing it to have been stolen, but he oecamped, and had been absent till the present time. A warrant was issued for his arrest. This was not his first appearance, be having been convicted in December, 1898, for theft of overcoats. The Bench committed the prisoner to gaol for a month with hard labour. Claoss CRUELTY TO A ManE.—John Barker was summoned for ill-treating a mare by working it whilst in an unfit state on the 31at of December.—lnspector Taylor stated that on the date In question, he was proceeding along Palmyra- square, and saw the defendant in charge of a grey mare attached to a lorry laden with coals. the animal was In such a poor condition that it was nothing but a frame. In turning into Suez street it almost fell down. On examina- Hoe he found that the mare was very lame in the near fore foot and the fetlock was very much swollen. It was also lame in the nest hind leg. Defendant said it lead been lame for about six weeks. There were 38cwts of coal on the lurry. Witness was of opinion that It was an act of gross cruelty to work the animal. It was in terrible pain.—A fine of 105 was</p>
        <p>imosed. A BURTONWOOD FARMER FlNED.—James Collier. a farmer, of Burtonwood, was summontd for causing an obstruction at Friars Green. by allowing a horse and cart to stand there on the previous Monday evening. —Defendant did not appear,— P.C. Whittaket said the house and cart were stanotng there for 3it hours. There was no light attached to the vehicle and as the defendant did not appear, he took the horse and cart to the Eagle and Child yard, Subsequently defendant turned up and it app .ared he had been drinking.—Fined and Costs. DRIVING WilmouT Rciss.—John Healy, a carter, lodgicg</p>
        <p>at Gleeaon's, Pereival'a Fold, was charged with driving a horse and cart without reins on the Bth January. —P.C. Cooper proves the ease.—Healy, who had nothing to say. was fined ss.</p>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions.</p>
      </div>
