      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>YES, IF THEY ONLY WOULD.</head>
        <p>YES, IF THEY ONLY WOULD.</p>
        <p>Dante defined man as " a thing that eats, driaks sleeps, and wears a coat." Any man can wear a coat who has got one to wear; but it is the necessity of eating, drinking, and sleeping that often makes it so hard to be a man.</p>
        <p>And yet here coines along a great and famous doctor who says, " IF men were bred like domestic animals, and if they would observe the elementary laws of physiology, disease would be almost unkno we." That sounds fine, and it is true ; but thee !—what's he use? People are not as well cared for as valuable dogs or horses are, and most of us pay no mere atten- tion to physiological idW9 than we do to the laws written on the tablets of ancient Babylon.</p>
        <p>Theretote we manufacture diseases in great variety, and have to cure them as best we can—loss of appotite and sleep among the rest. Right on this line the usefulness of Mother Seigel's Syrup shows up so clear and plain. Now such a case as this of Mrs. Charlotte Hawkesley, of 124, Fa wcett-street,Sheffield, is a common one,and all the more important because it is common. Some time ago she was troubled, so she says, with in- digestion ; which is often worse than a "trouble "—it is a deadly disease. Her tongue was covered with fur and tasted very bad of a morning. F_od seemed to stick in her chest, sad she usually had a feeling of oppression there ; yet it was worse after meals.</p>
        <p>In the nature of things we feel best and brightest after a ni.;ht in bed; bu: Mrs. Hawkesley did not. She was tired and languid even then. " After several months of this kind of thing," writes the lady on February 14th (1900), " I began using Seig,i's Syrup, and I can honestly say it completely cured me.</p>
        <p>" It made my food digest, it took away the tightness on the chest and the tired feeling, and gave me back my strength and vigor. I heartily recommend Seigel's Syrup to all who need a medicine." Mrs. E Mead, who lives az 17, Silk-street, Manning- ham, Bradford, had virtually the same experience, fol- lowed by the same good ending. _ .</p>
        <p>, " Since middle life," she says, " when I have been poorly I have never had to send for the doctor. for Mother Seigel'n'—as I call it—always puts me right. It is wonderful how much better I feel after one or two doses. It braces me up quickly." And this Seigel's Syrup helps people to eat, drink and Sleep and wear their clothes.</p>
        <p>Wirral and Birkenhead Agricultural Society. At meeting of the Executive Committee of this Society held on Tuesday last, it was unanimously resolved to bold the next Show at Birkenhead on Wednesday and Thursday the 4th and sth Septemlocr.</p>
        <p>LAMPS, Oil Stoves, Coke Stoves.—J. S. GABLUID,. 51 to 55, Bridge-street. Warrington.—Advt. Good Templary. The monthly meeting of the No 1 Convention S W Lancashire, of the Independent Order of Good Templars was held on Saturday, in the Templar Ashton-in-Makerfield. Bro Clarke, CC T (Earlestown), presided, representatives being pre- sent from Warrington, Earlestown, Ashton, Leigh, and the surrounding districts,—Bro E Newman, C Sec (Earlestown), presented his monthly report, which showed that most of the lodges were doing good work.—The report was adopted.—The Visiting Officers reported on their visits to the various lodges in their charge.—A discussion took place on the work of the Good Templar Order, and was ably par- ticipated in by Brothers G Clarke, C C T (Lades- town), E Newman, C Sec (Earlestown), J Sheath, C Mar (Warrington), and others. It was decided to hold the annual meeting at Warrington in February. Notes of sympathy were accorded to Bro Hulme (Leigh), on the death of his son, and Bro Fowler (Ashton), who is seriously ill, After tea a public meeting was held, when there was a good attend- ance. Bro G Clarke presided, and songs, recitation, Btc, were given by members and friends of the Order. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL YATASS Heatley Signalman Drowned. . .</p>
        <p>. - Mr; Robert Dobson, coroner, held an inquest at Lymm on Saturday on the body of John Williams, 33 years of age, who lived in Burford-lane, Lymm It appears that deceased left home on Wedneslay• night to go to work. He was not seen again, but his body was found in the canal on Friday.— Harriet Jane Williams said that deceased was her husband and was a signalman in the employ of the London and North-Western Railway Company, at the Sinderland crossing. He left home about 10.45 I on Wednesday, January 9th, to go to his work. She saw him go down the steps to the canal. She did not see him alive again. He told her that be intended to get a ride on a boat going in the direc- tion of Sinderland. There were a tug and three fiats going along the canal at the time deceased went. Benjamin Davies, Heatley, said he was a postman, and assisted in dragging the canal on January 11th. About 12.30 p.m, he found the body. It was quite near the Burford-lane bridge. He got it to the side and helped to carry it home. The overcoat was torn and there was a bruise on the head.—P.C. Sandbach, stationed at Broom Edge, Lymm, said he received information from the last witness as to the finding of the body and helped to take it home. The bottom part of the overcoat was torn and it was also torn elsewhere. There was a wound behind the right ear whicla appeared to have been inflicted by some blunt instrument. The mark was similar to those he bad seen which had been caused by the screw of a steam tug. Having heard of the Ideceased's appearance he made inquiries from the Bridgewater authorities, but could get no informa- tion as to anyone attempting to get on board a butt The night was dark and a rapid thaw had set in,_ The jury returned a verdict of "Found drowned."</p>
        <p>*,.. Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note the reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the Head Office, Bewsey-street. Warrington, not later than 10 o'clock on Thursday mornings or they cannot be inserted.</p>
        <p>Titer° 15 Seiturity GARTER 94 irrLE IVER Oman rill. Brnoll Dose PILLS Small Frio(' ; Ahsolutely Sirk H Biliousness, Li- Consv,,,on,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRP) V, JANUARY 18, 1901.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRP) V, JANUARY 18, 1901.</p>
        <p>from his own 25, ran in a try,and his brother landed the goal. Runcorn then had perhaps the better of toe game. Jolley soon dropped a goal. Richardson made an open- ing for Davie Jones, who was only prevented from scor- ing by being literally carried into touch-in-goal by Whitehead and Latham. Jolley later added five points on his own,scoring from a scrimmage close up, and con- verting. Firal:—Runcora, 5 :goals 4 tries (22 points) : Millom, 1 goal 2 tries (8 points) Oldham v. Swinton.</p>
        <p>The meeting between these old opponents took place at Oldham, and attracted a large assemblage. The tussle was a severe one, but the " Lions "triumphed, At the close of the initial half Swinton led by two goals (R Valentine) to one (9 Lees). Each side afterwards added a try (scored by Messer and Huzzey), thus leav- ing the Swintonians the victors by two goals and a try to a goal and a try. Salford v. Stockport.</p>
        <p>Salford, upon their own ground, managed to gain the lead during the course of the opening half, and retained it to the close, when they were declared the winners by two goals and a try to nil.</p>
        <p>Barrow v. Rochdale Hornets.</p>
        <p>The Hornets made a disarmainting display in this engagement, at Barrow, and only succeeded in scoring a goal, whilst the home side registered three goals and five tries, and so won easily.</p>
        <p>Widnes v. Broughton Rangers-</p>
        <p>Widnes, who played at Broughton, found their rivals too strong for them. The Rangers played well, and after having a substantial lead at the change over, they won by a goal and six tries to a try. Leigh v. St. Helens.</p>
        <p>At Leigh, the home &amp;election got the upper hand after a hard and fast game, and won by a goal and a try t " nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The Oldham-Swinton Match to be Replayed. _ _</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Lancaehire Senior Competition Committee was held at the Spread Eagle Hotel, Man- chester, on Tuesday night, when ehe protests in connec- tion with last Saturday's match between Oldham and Swinton at the Watersheddings were heard. On account of the crowd encroaching on the fiald of play, Swinton protested before the match, and afterwards Oldham lodged a formal objection to the game standing as a competition match on the same grounds. Messrs Yates and J Mills appeared for Swinton, and for Oldham Messrs Six maith and Miner submitted the following written statement which was accom- panied by a deposit of £5 as per bylaw 23 The Oldham Club asked the Competition Committee that the match be replayed, and that the money taken at the replayed match be divided on the principle of the Northern Union Cup-tiekor as the Committee deem fair. —The decision arrived at after a lengthy discussion was that the match be replayed at Oldham on Wednesday, January 23, the management of the match to be left in the hands of the Competition Committee, and the gate- money to be equally divided between the Lancashire Senior Competition and the Swinton and Oldham. clubs, Mr F Renton of Hunslet, was appointed referee, and Messrs J H Houghton (St. Helens) and W Jones (Run- , corn) touch judges. A complaint was made by Runcorn against Wigan for non-fulfilment of A" team fixtures. The Wigan Club was ordered to pay Runcorn the expenses incurred and to play another match at Runcorn. A complaint from the Salford Club was also heard against Wigan for simi- lar non-fulfilment of two A" team fixtures. The pre- vious resolution was adopted as applicable to the case. Leck, of Millom, WBB suspended for a fortnight for rough playe ; d Fisher,ndinohf Waioppanda, rfaourcae.similar offence, was suspendedanp gis</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Bradford v. Hull.</p>
        <p>In this match at Bradford the visitors were the first to score, Fildes crediting them with a try, which Parkinson converted. Wood, however, quickly retaliated with a try, which Marsden improvea upon. The last-named subsequently converted a try, gained by himself, and also landed a penalty goal for Bradford, who won by three goals and two tries to a goal and a try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Holbeck v:Leeds.</head>
        <p>Holbeck v:Leeds.</p>
        <p>This match, at Holbeck, was marred by an accident to Lumley, a membrer of the Leeds Club, who had the misfortune to break one of his legs. Holbeck won by a goal and two tries to a goal (Jagger). Hunslet v. Brighouse Rangers. Playing upon their own ground, tne Hunslet fifteen had much the better of the game, which ended in their favour by a goal and four tries to nil, a result chiefly due Ito the gocd play of Ramage, A Golthhorper Smith. and Lough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Liversedge v. Halifax.</head>
        <p>Liversedge v. Halifax.</p>
        <p>Some very keen play characterised this encounter at Liversedge. The visitors led by a try at the intervening period, but afterwards their opponents met with similar success, and a drawn game of throe points each was the result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Leeds Parish Church v. Castleford.</head>
        <p>Leeds Parish Church v. Castleford.</p>
        <p>Neither side was fully represented at Leeds,but in this respect the visitors suffered the most. The Churchmen won somewhat easily by three goals and two tries to nil. Brayshaw kicked the goals,and Evans and Foster gained the tries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Bramley v. Huddersfield. •</head>
        <p>Bramley v. Huddersfield. •</p>
        <p>Nothing was recorded until the second half of this game at Bramley, and then the home side ran in a try, which was converted. The vi4itors responded with a try, and lost by three points to five. Batley V. Wakefield Trinity.</p>
        <p>Play during the opening period at Bstley was very interesting, aad only three points sep.rated the two scores at the interval. Ia the end, however, Brantley won by two goals and two tries to a goal. Hull Kingston Rovers v. Manningham.</p>
        <p>All through this contest at Hull, the Rover; held the upper hand. At the intermediate stage they led by twa goals and a try to a try, and won by three goals and two tries to a try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LANCASHIRE "A" TEAMS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE "A" TEAMS COM- PETITION.</p>
        <p>Wigan "A "Team Disbanded.</p>
        <p>This match between Wigan A" and Warrington " A" wbict, ghould have Nicer' place at Wigan on Sat- urday, was cancellel I c)nseVence of the former club having been disbanded. Tho committees considered that this was the best course they could adopt, seeing that but few of the players could be relied upon to turn Up at matches played away from home.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH. Fletcher Russell's v. Runcorn "A.</p>
        <p>At Warrington on S.turday. The borne team at an e d 8 a ° vePealsYs' and Ashurst kicked a goal. and scored, the kick at goal A earlynt rosbtuagg i p interceptedress failing. Runcorn livened up after this reverse, and play was even. Butterworth scored for Runcorn, the kick at goal failing. Nixon afterwards scored. flail-time : Runcorn "A," six points; Fetcher Russell's, five. During the second haif the hotne team added a try, and thus won by eight points to alx.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.ack Society.</p>
        <p>There was an average attendance at the meeting of this society on Junday afternoon. Mr Harding presided and in a brief address he dwelt upon the importance of the members worthily maintaining the good name of the society by their conduct in every-d.y life.—Mr Ellames, of Manchester, delivered the address and referred to the progress which was made in the last century towards bringing the " classes " and the "masses" together, and , expressed the hope that still greater progress in this I direction would be seen in the present century. Mr. ' Perry, of Wigan, very efficiently sang two solos. The Rev J Yonge appealed for volunteers to undertake can- vassing work in connection with the forthcoming simultaneous mission in the town. The service was also taken part in by Mr John Atkinson and Mr Kent. Pound Service" at St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>On Sunday three sermons were preached in the above church in aid of the funds of the Church of England Incorporated Society for Providing Homes for Waifs and Strays. In the afternoon what is known as " a pound service " was held, most of the members of the congre- gation contributing a pound each of eitker rice, flour tea, &amp;c., which have since been seat to the St. George's Home off Church-street, Warrington, in which aecom- imodation is provided for eight boys. Each contribution I was wrapped in white paper, and during the singing of a couple of hymns the congregation left their seats and walked in procession to thu communion rails, where the gifts were handed over to the Vicar of the Parish (the Rev E R Tarbuck) and the special preacher for the day the Rev R Hawley Sharpe, of Seaforth, the clerical organizing secretary of the society. It was stated that there are over 2,800 poor and destitute children in the 86 homes of the society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Wycliffe Cockhedge Mission.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Cockhedge Mission.</p>
        <p>The members of the women's sewing class in connec tion with the above Mission were entertained to tea on Monday evening. There were about 60 friends and mem- . .</p>
        <p>bars present and they were favoured with the presence of their late pastor —the Rev J Davies—and his wife, After tea the tables were removed and the evening spent in an enjoyable manner. Songs, recitations, &amp;c., were given, the duties of accompanist being performed by Miss K Hall. The proceedings were presided over by Mrs Butterworth, and a pleasant evening was brought to a conclusion by the singing of the Doxology.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Buckley-street Methodist Free Church.</head>
        <p>Buckley-street Methodist Free Church.</p>
        <p>Special revival meetings have been held during the past fortnight in connection with the above place of worship, by Mr W Longworth (the converted collier, of Elaydock). All the meetings have been well attended, and about 50 persons have signified their adherence to the cause through the earnest and untiring efforts of the missioner,and he has,in consequence of his great success, decided to remain another three weeks. Mr Long- worth preaehed special sermons on Sunday, the con- gregations being very large. On Monday evening, Ue missioner, who was listened to with rapt attention, gave a description of his life and conversion. Councillor Brough occupied the chair on the latter occasion.</p>
        <p>United Evangelical Communion Service.</p>
        <p>Oa Sunday evening the annual united communion in connection with the Evangelical Free Churches of War- rington was held in the Emmanuel Church, Bold-street. Previous to the service the pulpits fat the evening ser- vice) of the following churches had 'been inter-changed, as follows : Bewsey-roall Wesleyan, Rev J T Schofield; Bold-street Wesleyan, Rev J F Matthews ; Emmanuel Reformed Episcopal, Rev John Yonge ;-Golborne-street Baptist, Rev Theodore Bishop ; Latchford Baptist, Rev C Finch • Legh-street Primitive Methodist, Rev E T Reed ; WYcliffs Congregational, Rev. C H Hocken. The communion service was well attended, the following ministers beine present :—Revs E T Reed, T Bishop, C Finch, C U Hocken, J F Matthews, J L Milne, J T Schofield, T Williams. J Yonge and J Gunnell. There was also a good attendance of members of the various congregations attending the chapels. Before the ad- ministration of the Lord's supper an address was given by the Rev C Finch. The service closed with the Bene- diction:</p>
        <p>Holy Trinity Church. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Annual Tea Party and Entertainment.</p>
        <p>This annual event took place in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening and passed off with success. The room had been prettily decorated for the occasion with flags, bunting, Japanese fans, Sco. A large number of parishioners and friends sat down to tea, lady members of the congregation assisting at the tables. After tea,an entertainment was held, the chair being onoupied by Councillor Porshaw. who was supported on the platform by the Vicar (Rey E CE Carleton), the Revs H R Wild and Carter and Mr J Halton (warden). The Vican, in the course of a short resume of the year's work, said they had bad a satisfactory year, and cheeks to the help of some kind friends, whose liberality never seemed to flag year by year, they could still hold up their heads and look their creditors—he meant their church creditors—in the face. But, although they could do this,they must not run away with the idea that they had anything to spare, for he believed that when Easter came round they would see from the balance sheet pub- lished that income and expenditure about balanced each other and no more. In fact at the present moment he believed the finances of the wardens were just a trifle weaker than they were this time last year, owing to a circumstance that he need not now enter upon. He was sure it was a great grief to the churchwardena, as it was to himself, that they could not always res- pond to appeals on behalf of excellent institutions in the town or in the diocese or the country that were con- stantly made. But they felt that the greatest care and discretion must be exercised if the finances of the parish were not to become altogether disorganized. There ware only b 2 Sundays its the year, and he believed their treasurer could tell them at once how many Of these Sunday collections were required to support the , church, and how many could be spared for the various charitable institutions of the town and the diocese. Reughly speaking, they were able to give the collections of one Sunday in each month for some special purpose, and if they attempt,cl to give more it would mean a deficit of £4. or £5 on the working expenses account for every Sunday given away. He believed their church worshippers always did their share towards the suppoit of the general charities of the town and the diocese,and it more was to be done there ought to be some plan set on foot for getting tbe contributions of those who did not attend church at all; who by their non-attendance missed many opportunities of taking an interest in and assisting the various charitable institutions of the town and district that needed support. He wished to thank the treasurer of the Sunday school for allowing the pro- ' ceeds of this tea party to go towards meeting the loss which the St Luke's Curate Fund bad suffered during the year. The loss was owing to a part of the endowment of the church being invested in Great Central Stock,and the Great Central Line bad not been able to pay a dividend upon all its preference sharesond they were among the sufferers. Ho hoped the loss was only tem- porary, but meanwhile it was inconvenient for the treasurer. Mr. Carleton concluded by thanking several of the church workers for their support during the year. An attractive musical programme was given. A piano forte duet entitled " Qui Viva" was given by Miss Marian an Mr Percy Randall ; songs were sung by Mrs G fault and Mrs Williams: duets and songs by Messrs G Parr, T Amos, and Healing. Mr Hallard scored a dis- tinct success as an entertainer, his humorous sketches and songs being given in inimitable style. He was several times encored. The entertainmeut concluded with selections on the graphaphone which had been lent for the occasion by Mr J Roberts of Fearnhead. The proceedings concluded with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
