      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPE TITION.</p>
        <p>Warrington's Lucky Win.</p>
        <p>The match at Wilderspool on Saturday was with the ill-fated Wigan team, whom Warrington easily defeated at Wigan in the early stages of the Competition. Though poorly represented, Isherwood being absent in addition to Fi.th and the injured players, Warrington thought they had a sure victory in prospect. As events turned out they had the narrowest squeak imaginable and only won by a single point. Wigan brought a full team and were evidently determined to win if they pos- sibly could. Fine weather favoured the fixture and the ground was in good order. Mr Hancock, Runcorn, was the referee, and the teams lined out as follows. Warrington : J Hallam, full back ; Ratcliffe,Hocken- hall, Dickenson, and Harris, three-quarters ; Burgess and R Bate, half-backs ; Taylor, Boardman, Scholtze, Eden, Fairhurst, Swift. Pemberton, and Cunningham, forwards. Wigan : Anderton, full-back; Harris, Owen, Eckers- ley and Barr, three-quarters; Nelson and Middleton, half- backs; Prescott, Brown, Hall, Halliwell, Yates, Collier, Rigby, and Hilton, forwards. Taylor started for Warrington against a light breeze, the Wiganers being the first to get possession from the scrimmage. Nelson passed to Middleton, but the latter spoiled the movement by a bad transfer. A point from Nelson was well followed up and Hallam was grassed in his own quarter, Wigan thus early taking up the attack. Warrington defended well, and at last changed the venue through Harris, who ran and punted smartly into the Wig an h atf. Dickensan clear of the Wigan backs and a race for the line ensued, Hock- enhall and Dickenson both being robbed of what was a sure try, by deliberate obstruction. A penalty kick was awarded but nothing came of it. Burgess dropped at goal while Warrington were on the attack, but be failed to score from the easiest of positions. Wigan worked their way to the centre again where Harris tried to lower the home goal but was unsuccessful. A few min- utes later he had another shot and sent the ball over the bar with a very fine kick. This success encouraged the Wiganers who played with great resolution and kept Warrington penned up. Nelsaa got away cleverly and passed back near the line. Rigby was at hand and romped over under the posts with an easy try. Harris added the major points and Warrington found them- selves seven points in arrear. Even play followed to the interval. Barr only just failing to add to the visitors, score after a strong run down touch. The second half opened with Wigan still in an ag- gressive mood, and Warrington had to defend. En- couraged by the shouts of the home crowd and the des- ' perateness of the situation Warrington livened up and drove Wigan back by ashing forward play. Anderton was captured near the line and Warrington attacked with spirit. Burgess broke away but passed badly, and time went by with the score still unaltered. Warrington got pessession oftener and a passing movement by Bate and Dickenson saw Harris in possession. The wing man ran strongly, and by a fine effort got over at the corner, amid the cheers of the crewd. Hallam failed to augment. Warrington's chance was now much brighter and the team still playing with groat dash, Wigan were hard pressed. Radcliffe led another attack and took a free kick near the goal, but misjudged the angle slightly. Wigaa obtained relief by means of a penalty, but Warrington were soon back again playing with desperate energy; The ball came out on the home side and Dickenson got possession. Running strongly he neared the line and drawing his opponents on to Ratcliffe he dodged over cleverly and scored himself near the posts. Ratcliffe took the kick which was either to win or lose the match and placed a nice goal. The crowd signified their jay at the home team's lead Warrington were the best team to the clue and attacked persistently without result. The Wigan defence was very good. Final :—Warrington one goal, 2 tries, (8 points), Wigan 2 goals, I try (7 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Spirited Game at Runcorn.</head>
        <p>A Spirited Game at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Runcorn entertained Millom on Saturday last, at frwell-lane, and though the final result was very much in favour of the honsesters, the game was not by any :neans a one-sided one. As a matter of fact, it was very evenly and spiritedly contested. But to the details of the encounter. Both teams were representative, with the slight difference that Runcorn were minus their usual jerseys. donning ,t white " instead of their favourite green, on account of the visitors dark blue, being too akin to the well-known bottle colour. There was but a small gate, due more to the fact that it was a bleak raw afternoon, rather than to the anticipation of easy vic- tory, though no one for a moment doubted the result. Starting in lively fashion, the game continued for a few moment] with slight advantage to Millom. Then the ball was carried from end to end principally by the keen exertions of both sets of forwards. li.ushton defended well, and at length with one of his character- istic dashes secured the advantage to his side. Following a capital passing bout Davie Jones ran in a fine try plac- ing the ball downright between the sticks. Jolley bad an easy task and converted. Millom were by no means over-played, and soon gave Houghton evidence of the fact. Their visit might hava proved successful if. D Jones had clot 13,111.0 Arn with a tine effort alone ` collared, however '</p>
        <p>of the field. He</p>
        <p>the top side . ....G. He got nowever, by Whitehead, but from the scrummage Jolley picked up and dashed over. From an awkward position Jolley again secured the extra points. Another visit to Houghton found that player obliged to touch death Yet that was but the first of a series of attempts by Millom, which ended in Lattiam scoring a beautiful try, after running half the length of the field. In his course he beat both Jones and Houghton. The eff 3rt was loudly and deservedly applauded. After that success the game became a pleasure to watch, and for a time really good football was displayed. Later Wilkinson marked and Jolley landed a third goal. Al the interval Runcorn stood three goals, three tries; Millom, one try. The visitors recommenced and for some time the game was as even as cGuld be,the ball being sent froin hand to hand in delightful fashion. Yet the tackling was so keen none of the backs could travel far before being " held up." Each get of forwards was again prominent, their passing and following up being grand. When the fun was at its height Sam Walker accomplished an astonishing feat. Intercepting a pass intended for Latham, he raced off from about halfway and beat Whitehead on the line. Jolley failed to land this goal, but it is worthy of note that it was the only kick he failed at throughout the match. Although a long way behind, Millom still con- tinued to play delightfully. J Whitehead breaking away</p>
      </div>
