        <head>JACOB ATTR I LL.</head>
        <p>JACOB ATTR I LL.</p>
        <p>The letters we receive from people who have used Scott's Emulsion show clearly that this preparation succeeds in giving relief where other remedies do no good whatever. In writing us people frequently mention the fact that they had tried many other remedies but were disappointed in them. The result is that they have :dread expended more or less money fruitlessly, and have expressed regret that they had not taken Scott's Emulsion at the out- set of the treatment. Here is the case of a child suffering from a troublesome cough for which various remedies were tried without any benefit : " 2, Richmond Grove, Surbiton Hill, " Surbicon Surrey, "April 3rd, 1899. "Dear Sirs,—l am pleased to say we have found Scott's Emulsion beneficial in every way for our little son. Last summer he underwent a very serious operation with an abscess in the lung, which left him with a very troublesome cough. I tried nearly everything to ease him, but nothing did him good until my uncle told me of Scott's Emulsion ; and then I sent for a sample bottle, and have con- tinued it ever since. I c msider it a wonderful medicine, and I have recommended it to a lot of</p>
        <p>remedies is at best an unsatisfactory process. They cost money which might much better be put into a standard prep4ration that is known to physicians and has won its high place on its merits alone. Scott's Emulsion differs from all other cod-liver oil preparations in its form and the nature and propor- tion of its ingredients. It contains only the highest grade medicinal Norway cod-liver oil and completely disguises its taste and makes it easy of digestion. People are finding out every day, by hard experi- ence with other remedies, that there is something about Scott's Emulsion that will do the work</p>
        <p>required of it. In coughs, colds, bronchitis, and other local affections of throat and lungs, Scott's Emulsion is very effectual, both as a specific and a nutrient to give strength, and it is the best remedy that can be employed in any phase of wasting or emaciation. Thousands are being benefited daily by Scott's Emulsion, and these cases include not only affeo. tions of throat and lungs, but various diseases where it is essential to enrich the blood,promote the making of flesh and give vitality. A sample of Scott's Emulsion will be forwarded to any address in Great Britain, if the applicant will kindly mention the name of this paper and send threepence in stamps to cover postage,to Scott and Bowne, Limited, 95, Great Saffron Hill, London, E.C.</p>
        <p>-- my frien.ds here in Surbiton. I shall always recom- mend it when I get the ebance.—Believe me, Yours (Signed) K. ATTEILL." Experimenting with unknown and untried</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Carter Bell, A.R.S.M., &amp;c., Analyst to the County of Chester, reports : "I have analysed a sample of Messrs. Thomas Foden and Co.'s Horse and Cattle Food and I find it to be of excellent feeding quality, a splendid tonic, and free front any adulteration." (Signed) J. CARTER BELL, A.R.S.M., &amp;c. 303. per Cwt, Carriage Paid.</p>
