        <p>(6205.) ORDER OF THE BOARD OF AGRICULTURE. (DATED 2ND JANUARY, 1901). (bWINE-FEVER INFECTED Aim.) THE BOARD OF AGRICULTURE, by virtue 1 and in exercise of the pow eta in them vested under the Diseases of Animals Acts, 1894 and 1896, and of every other power enabling them in this behalf do erdeto and it is hereby ordered, as follows :- 1. The Area described in the Schedule to this Order is hereby declared to be an area infected with swine- fever for the purposes of the Swine-Fever (Infected Areas) Order of 1896. 2. This Order shall come into operation on the Ninth day of January, one thousar.d ninu hundred and one. In witness whereof the Board of Agriculture have hereunto set their Official Seal this Second day of coo thousand trine hundred and one.</p>
        <p>(Signed)</p>
        <p>T. H. ELLIOTT, SJcretary;</p>
        <p>SCHEDULE. An Area comprising the petty sessional divisions of Leigh, Warrington, Childwail, Present (including ift! detached portion), St. Helens (including its cletachea portion),and Wigan (except the parish of Haigh), and the parish of Garston, in the administrative county of Lancaster ; and also comprising the boroughs of 56 Helens, Leigh, Warrington, Widnes, and Wigan. published by order of the Board. AND TAKE NOTICE that by the Swine-Fever (Ia: fccted Areas) Order, 1896, Swine may noc moved Within, out of, or into an infected area Licence granted upon a Declatation made in with such Order.</p>
