      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>POOR CHILDREN'S TREAT.</p>
        <p>Tea and Entertainment in the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>To many poor children into whose homes even the joys of the Christmas season do not penetrate to any appreciable degree, the Christmas treat, which through the thoughtfulness and generosity of a number of townspeople, is held annually in the Parr Hall, naturally comes as a ray of sunshine into their lives. It is three years since this philanthropic work was mooted, and the efforts of the promoters have been amply rewarded by the enjoyment which the treat has afforded to the children who have had the good fortune to participate in it. This year's treat was held in the Parr Hall, on Thursday last week, and, thanks to the devoted and unselfish labours of those who took upon themselves the task of collecting funds, and attending to the wants of the children,the event was attended with the com- plete success which was to be desired. The arrange- ments bad been carried out by a strong ladies' com- mittee, under the presidency of the Mayoress, and with Messrs H Taylor and W 11 J Jenkins as hon. secretary and treasurer, and a mead of praise is due to the two gentlemen named,for the indefatigable manner in which they worked to ensure the success of the treat. The tickets had been distributed to those deserving of them, a fortnight ago, and the recipients had, no doubt, ever since then been anxiously longing for the great day to arrive. The hour at which the tea was served was half-past five, but long before that time, the children who numbered about twelve hundred had assembled outside the hall, with faces full of expectancy. The spectacle of this assembly occupying the whole of the body of the hall was one of a most interesting and animated character, and when the meal com- menced, it is hardly necessary to state that the youngsters attacked the abundance of good things provided, with avidity. The repast consisted of meat pies, Eccles cakes, buns and tea, the latter being generously supplied by Mr Monro. The platform had been very tastefully decorated, the mottoes "A happy New Year," "God bless our Queen,"" Do right and fear not "and "A hearty welcome " being displayed in the background. Three large Christmas trees heavily laden with toys &amp;c. were prominent, and other toys intended for distribution to the children were arranged on the platform. After the wants of the youngsters had been satisfied, the hall was prepared for the entertainment which had been arranged. Prior to the commencement of this ' interesting part of the proceedings, the Mayor Alderman Roberts) addressed a few words to the gathering, He said he would call their attention to one motto on the screen," Do right and fear not." He wanted them to remember it not only during the present year, but through all their lives, and if they did right and feared not they had no need to be afraid but that they would attain success, He concluded his remarks by asking for cheers for all who had worked to contribute to the enjoy- ment of the proceedings, and the request was voci- ferously complied with. The entertainment then proceeded, all the items being of a character which specially appealed to the youthful mind. The conjuring, ventriloquial, and Punch and Judy per- formances by Professor Shelly, of Manchester, were very cleverly carried out to the immeasurable de- light of the yoangsters, whose smiling countenan- ces betokened their appreciation of the entertain- ment. The other portion of the programme was creditably sustained by the pupils of the Misses Rimmer, Mr P Randall presiding at the organ. At the conclusion the children were marched on the platform and were presented by the Mayoress and other ladies with toys. Before leaving the hall each received a packet of sweets or an orange. A number of stewards, including Mr Sir Hackett, N.S.P.C.C. Inspector, Mr W Jones and other school attendance officers, rendered valuable assistance, and on the whole the children conducted them- selves in a very orderly manner The National Anthem having been sung, thelgathering dispersed there being a COCICHLIMIIg of opinion that the trea had been the most enjoyable one yet held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>WARRINGTON MAUISTRATES AND</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MAUISTRATES AND</p>
        <p>VACCINATION.</p>
        <p>Strong Remarks to Anti-Vaccinationists. At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Thursday, last week before Alderman Fairclough and Mr J R Young, Edward Harrison, Birchal -street, made an application for exemption from vaccination of his child, Ernest Edward Harrison—The Chair- man asked Harrison what his objection was —Applicant said he was of opinion that vaccination did no good to thechild. He had read many books on the subject, and had attended 'echroical clasaes: Lord Playfair could not guarantee the lymph pure. He (applicant) had had two children, and the one which had been vaccinated caught an infection first.— Mr J B Young: Da you put your opinion before that of the medical profession of this country?—Applicant : The , medical profession is no more perfect now than it was in the time of Adam. (Laughter.) Mr Young : Do you know as much about the matter as the medical gentlemen P—Applicant : I don't know, but I daresay I could answer a few questions. I have read about the matter, and attended tanning classes, and understand the skin and bow it contracts diseases.—Mr Young; If a medical man talked about tanning as you do about their profession, don't you think he would be unreason- able P—Applicant : Perhaps ; but the majority of doctors don't believe in vaccination.— alderman Fran- dough: Well, there are two doctors here in court ;, I'll ask them what they think about it. Now Dr. Bowdon and Dr. Naden let's have your opinion atrmi the mater —Dr. Bowden : All I can say is that I have been re vaccinated five times.—Dr. Naden : And I have been re-vaccinated also,—Alderman Fairclough : It is the stupidity of these people which makes them come here. The last smallpox epidemic in Warrington cost the town £lO.OOO and these stupid people come here in spite of that.—Mr Young, (to tee applicant) : We distinctly think you are taking a pre' judiced view of it, and we think your o bjection is trivolous, It is one of those cases where a man does himself injury through ignorance.—The in agistrates eventually granted the application.—Another applica. tion by Thomas Ward, Maybury-street, was also granted Alderman Fairclough remarking that such people did'ot care a rap for their children, but it was only from a desire for notoriety, or else their stupidity,whidh caused them to act thus. They wanted to be a little different from anyone else.—Mr Young : The most serious p rt of it is that it endangers society and indicts harm on members of society who do believe in vaccination'</p>
      </div>
