      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>IL Mug to tte Obitor.</head>
        <p>IL Mug to tte Obitor.</p>
        <p>Entertainment for the Masses.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l wonder how it is that we have not more amusement provided in Warrington for the young people? It is a pity the way some of them walk about the streets at night, nowhere to go and nothing to do, except stare about. I think :he example of the Cairo- street Unitarian Chapel authorities ought to be more widely followed- They have most kindly and generous- ly opened their schoolroom on certain nights of the week, to the young folk, and provitled them with ample entertainments. During the past festive season I have been struck forcibly with this question, as, barring the theatre, there was nothing in the way of entertainment provided for the bulk of the masses. Thanking you for publication, yours truly, H. THOMAS: Warrington, Jan. 10, 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Trade Dispute at Messrs Crosfield's.</head>
        <p>The Trade Dispute at Messrs Crosfield's.</p>
        <p>Sir,—A letter appeared in your correspondence column last Saturday from Mr Copley containing one or two rtatementa which I should like to correct.</p>
        <p>Referring to your report of his speech made on December 23rd, Mr Copley says " it reads as if Mr Dale had only just commenced to interrupt when I was speak- ing. As a matter of fact he tried all he could to disturb the meeting before I commenced to speak. If he had but listened to what was said, at the close of my address he would have had an opportunity to put whatever ques- tion he felt disposed to, but his zeal ran away with his discretion, thinking, I suppose, that be could come and take possession of something he had no right to, so he has only himself to blame for what was said." Mr. Copley is evidently seeking for some kind of justi- fication for certain very strong remarks that he made at the meeting in question. It so happens that he is once more mistaken. The statement that I tried to disturb the meeting before he began to speak is false, as I did not utter a single word during the meeting until Mr Copley's attack on a gentleman sitting near me who had simply called oult."`No " whilst Mr Copley was speaking Pointing to the gentleman he described him as a " Cad " and remarked with vehement emphasis "If a hole were dug 10,000 miles deep and he and others like him were buried in it it would be better for„the world," and then rising to a pitch of still greater excitement he continued "If this meeting is to bothered with a " cad "if there is a long fingered man present to help this firm to do wrong, I ask this meeting to protest. There may be such men here who are anxious to climb over innocent workmen's shoulders in order to get a higher position : we have come here to speak the truth." I then called out "You are talking at random." With this exception I did net utter a word until after the conclusion of the address. In your article on this question you very fitly describe Me. Copley's language as iafiammatory. Following upon this style of speech there appeared quite a touch of humour in the remark that he was often complimented for his courtesy. Mr. Copley had better search in some other direction if he is anxious to find an excuse to minimize the extravagance of his semi-hysterical utter- ances. Yours truly, January 10th, 1901.</p>
        <p>G. DALE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Borough Auditors' Report. Mr. Pemberton Determined.</head>
        <p>The Borough Auditors' Report. Mr. Pemberton Determined.</p>
        <p>Sir—l have read a report of Tuesday's Council Meet- ing with some surprise. In the first place I am thunder struck that our Town Council is SO loosely managed that a committee has the power to suppress such an impor- tant matter as the Auditors' report without the consent of the Town Council, and such rules ought to be at once passed as would make it impossible in the future. The Chairman's statement eould not well be weaker and he ends up by saying "I am not saying that there may not be many things that the Auditors could at times wisely bring to our notice. But I strongly object to the in- troduction of matters of this simple kind in the report, which should be an important one" Mind,he admits there are many things that should I be brought forward.but what has been brought forward is teo simple. And, ye Gods ! look what took place on the simple ones being brought forward ; one of the auditors was insulted, and no gentleman who had it in his power would run the risk of such another scene. I have heard very many expressions of sympathy for Mr Councillor Bennett and most of the public are pleased and thank him very much for his manly stand. There is too little chance of open and free dis- cussion in our Town Council, in fact most of the Town Council meeting is a simple farce. The law made Council meetings public so that any councillor who was not on a certain com- mittee could, when the minutes of that committee came before the Council, ask what questions he thought desir- able and the public and the press would be there to see the merits or otherwise of the action of that committee. This used to be so in Warrington and at that time the General Purposes Committee usee to consist only of Chairmen of Comnaittees. But this,like many good old customs has been done away with and now the General Purposes Committee consists of the whole of the Town Council and it bas this effect. A member of the Coun- cil who is going to bring any matter before the Town Council has to give so many days' notice of motion, the matter is then brought up before the General Purposes Committee, which of college sits in private (no reporters are present and whatever reports are published are just so much RS the committee makes up its mind that the public should know). Now this motion may be most important and of great public interest and some of our councillors may fight against something which they think our-ht not to be done in the best interest of the town, others may think otherwise and on voting may pats a resolution pledging the town. Now mark what follows, all this eau and has been kept from the public in this way, At ,he monthly meeting of the Town Council, the chairman of the General Purposes Committee get up and moves :hat the minutes of that committee be pulsed, someone seconds that and the thing is done. Ido not wonder that the Warringten press is able to state (as it has done on several occasions) that the Town Council meeting on such a day there was nothing of public interest and the meeting was over in so many minutes. All that wants altering. We want no committee with the whole Council in it because it can meet in private and if a member wants to bring anything to light at a Town Council meeting he is met with the argument that he had had his chance at the General Purposes Committee meeting. Jost so, how nice and convenient, no reporter present. In reference to our Mayor's remarks as to the " scurrilous and contemptible insinuations which had been conveyed to the press by certain persons in the town who would be better employed in looking after matters more directly concerning themselves, and in regard to which there was room for considerable improvrment." I am one of those perseas, and I was never more astonished in my life to read that such lan- guage had fallen from the lips of our Mayor. I think he is making a mistake, and I consider that our town affairs do concern me very much. I am a large rate paver and have to get my living in the town. I may inform him I am on this business, and will not be per- suaded nor knocked off it until it is right through._ Yours truly, JOHN PEMBERTON. January 11th,1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB V. PENICETII.—Last Saturday evening. the members of the Runcorn Liberal Club entertained a renregentative team from Penketh Liberal Club, in a friendly billiard encounter, with the following ref'ult</p>
        <p>RUNCORN. PETICETB. A 13 Parr lOO T Barker T Worrell l.lO Bradstreet 47 W B Brimelow W Lawton 3 Bawlinson H Chadwick • lOO R Kerr T Cooke lOO V Dale A Green 12 Hughes lOO C Love . . . 46</p>
        <p>W Riley J Joyce B7 W Surnn.r T Yates lOO R Routlege .</p>
        <p>1187 838 IrCEIESTER V. RtlNCOßN.—Tearng representing the re- spection Liberal clubs of the above places,met at Chester, on Wedrosday, with the following result</p>
        <p>CHESTER. RUNCORN. P Williams H Wright lOO W B Brirnelow C Stewart lOO R Owen 37 H Davies G H Crawford P Griffiths 3 Walsh 3 Mayers J Lunn lOO R Walton ... 66 T G Hyde W A Nixon lOO Williams H Thomas 872</p>
        <p>- 1169</p>
        <p>Hockey.—Latchford v Flixton. The above teams met for their first engagment at Flixton, and a pleasant game ensued except for the fog, which was very dense during one portion of the game. From the bully off the visitors rushed down, and Grounds scored in the first few minutes. After some play in midfield the visitors again pressed,and from a fine pass from Pearson Grounds secured a second goal. During the second half Banks, the visitors' right wing,made some splendid runs. Nothing further was scored on either side, and so the whistle sounded, Latchford winning by two to nil.</p>
