      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>EXAMINER' FRIDAY, JANUARY 18 1901.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER' FRIDAY, JANUARY 18 1901.</p>
        <p>LATEST. NEWS. British Warship Wrecked.</p>
        <p>The Crew Saved.</p>
        <p>The Press Association telegraphs that Lloyd's agent at Capetown cables ! UNITARY 17th. 11.50 a.m.</p>
        <p>British warship Sybille reported wrecked Lambert's Bay. Crew saved. The Sybille is a twin- screw second-class cruiser and carries eight guns* The Admiralty confirm the report of the dis- aster to the Sybille, but add that the damage was not great.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS. Boers Severely Defeated.</p>
        <p>The War Office yesterday afternoon issued the following from Lord Kitchen,,r oszPRETORIA, 16T11 JANUARY. Concentration 3,000 B)ers at Carolina reported. Colville's motile column was engaged near Van- tonder's Hoek. Boers were driven off with heavy loss. Small party of Boers entered Aberdeen yesterday morning and looted the stores.</p>
        <p>One hundred infantry with some mounted men arrived at 4.45. Three hundred B3ers fell back on'seeing our troops.</p>
        <p>Perjured Boer Sentenced to Death.</p>
        <p>A Boer named EClekoemoer who was found in possession of arms after t skin the oath of neu- trality was triel at Johannesburg on WeJnesday and sentenced to death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Captain Crosfield on his Way Hone</head>
        <p>Captain Crosfield on his Way Hone</p>
        <p>Mr and Mrs John Crosfield, who are still on a visit to Folkstone, on Monday night received a cablegram stat- ing that Captain George Crosfield, who has been invalided horno, will sail this week in the Tantallon Castle. In a letter dated the 24th December, he stated that he was much better, and able to get about, though still very weak from the results of the enteric fever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRE</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRE</p>
        <p>VOLUNTEERS IN NATAL.</p>
        <p>No Work for the Doctor.</p>
        <p>Swimming Gala at Sundays River.</p>
        <p>The South Lancashire Volunteer Service Company determined that if they were permitted to spend Christmas on Sundays River in the company of their own comeany,which they naturally prefer to any other, they would make it as enjoyable and as mercy a day as possible- This, however, was not to be, and the officer , commanding received orders to move into Ladysmith on December 20th. One of the items on our Christmas pro gramme was a swimming gala, which we determined, however, to have on Tuesday, December 18th. Imagine if you can while shivering in Lancashire, a December 18th so hot that the rocks blistered your bare feet and the sand made you do knees up down the river bank in double time. The flies evidently thought a cannibal festi- val was taking place,and came in large numbers to feast on the roasting and blistered backs and necks of South Lancashire Volunteers ; and fearing that the Boers might also arrive to join in the feast in revenge for all their babies which they thought the " Rooinek Roasters" had devoured, we all went down to the river. We have simply lived in the water here and donned with great regret even our scanty uniform of shirt, trousers and hat, which has been the regulation kit when off duty. We got up a small sick parade each morning to satisfy the conscience of our worthy doctor (Dr Gilroy) a civilian doctor" from Birmingham," whose work mainly consisted in pulling. out a toothior two, or care- fully examining a bruised big toe. He, however, smil- ingly administered a golden pill to the whole company toward the swimming and kept remarking to the I captain," You know I am no use here," and to look at the brown and healthy anatomy of the company one could not help agreeing with him. We had a first class bath in a long deep stretch of the river above the bridge for experts, with diving boards, &amp;c, and a second class bath in a stretch of the river about five feet detp below the bridge, where those who could only swim a little struggled to improve themselves, and the non-swimmers soon began to chuck away the non— in this they were encouraged and helped by their more accomplished comrades. We have read with cheeks blushing beneath our bronze of the preparations being made f w our reception in Warrington, and we venture to suggest to the committee still another item on their programme, namely, that en route from Bank Quay Station to Orford Barracks the company should swim in fours from Warrington Bridge up the silver Mersey to Captain Edelsten's Works, where he would doubtless allow us to retire for a sh-irt time to change our kit and to partake of a wee drop of Scotch to keep out the cold for auld Lang syne. The following is the programme of events:—</p>
        <p>Costume Race.—Each man to swim about 60 yards in helmet, tunic, trousers, and boats : 1, Private W Ashton; 2, Private T Lowe. Second Class Race.—Fiftv yards, swam in three heats: 1. Private W Pendlebury ; 2, Bugler Sephton ; 3, Lance- Cornoral Ma9sey.</p>
        <p>Novices' Race.—Acrosa the river and back aqain Private Tl 3 Adieu 2, Private Dakin ; 3, Private Lowcock.</p>
        <p>First Class Race.—One hundred yards handicap : 1, Private W Ashton, giving 20 seconds; 2, Lance-Corooral J Fishwick, giving 12 seconds ; 3, Private T Lowe. giving 12 seconds. Neat Diving ICompetition.-1, Private T Lowe; 2, (Privates J E Beath, Higham and Marsden). Sectional Squadron Race.-1, giving 40 seconds, No 2 section, Privates Ashton, Lowe, 7045 F liughes,Thonaas, J Allen, and Higbam ' -2, No 4 section, Lieutenant Robson, Lance-Corporals Massey and Mercer ; Privates J E Booth Pendlebury ani Ratcliffe- Grand Polo Match, under N.S.A. and South African Rules. Fishwick's Team v Ashton's Team.—Fishwick's, team : Lance-Corporal Fishwick (captainl.7o4s Privates F Hughes, W Pendlebury, S Ratcliffe' 7023 J W Taylor (goal), Byrne and Woodward. Ashton's team : Privates W Ashton (captain), T Lowe, Lieutenant E Robson, Lance-Corporal J Leo (goal), Private T Bate, Sergeant Dutton, Privates J E Booth, and 11 Eligham. F arhhwe i pc kr o' agrt eaammmwe ownagb y csaervrei end goouatl s twoi fourth th eg outs s. good temper of the "Merry men," so truly christened such by Major Adam, who knows thorn well. The costume race was most amusing, and was no easy task, especially for those taking part in the subsequent events. The dripp- ing heroes were photographed by Lieutenant Lewis and the doctor on reaching terra firma. The novices race was easily won by Frivate T B Allen, who has now left that class for ever. The second class race was very well contested, and was won after a hard struggle by Private Pendleburv. The first class race wa won in magnificent style by Private W Ashton though heavily handicapped, and all agreed that he, who is undoubtedly the finest swimmer in the com- pany, thoroughly deserved it, and it was no mean feat to beat two Such good men as Lance-Corporal Fishwick, and Private T Lowe. Private F Hughes was a very good fourth. The diving competition, for which there were over twenty entries, was very hard to judge as the divine was very good throughout, and there was very little to choose between the four men receiving prizes and several others who ran them very close. The sectional squadron race was well contest-d, but there ' was little doubt that the best section won. The polo match, though perhaps not first CIaSS polo, was most amusing and caused much fun to the teams and spec- tators, and wi'l never be forgotten by either the one or the other. Lieutenant Lewis acted as M. 0., and the company thoroughly appreciated his energy and work and honest endeavour to make the gala the succ'ss it undoubtedly was. The photographers were basy during the day and the polo teams were duly taken, and we hope to be able to show the results to our friends at home. One photo (possibly for private circulation only) was a group of some of our most stalwart men in Kaffir full dress, which seemed to cause tremendous amuse- ment to the real article looking on, as it did to the white spectators also. A stiff dose of hot rum punch in the evening, we trust neutralised any ill effect from the long immersion of the competirora.—Sunday's River. Bridge, Natal, 20'h December, 1900." Ladysmith, 22nd December, 1900.—The above account of a rather amusing swimming gala we had while guarding Sundaes River Bridge had to be written under difficult circumstances yesterday, as it was rain- ing " cats and dogs " all the night before and all day, and in addition to this we had to strike camp and come on here. We were very happy at Sundays River and could live there in any kit, but here we have to ba more particular and have the less pleasant task of guarding Boer prisoners, who are arriving at the rate of 200 a day, Our company is on guard to-day, DRLTCSOUS MAZAWATTRR TEA. DELICIOUS MAZXWATTRIi TEA DELICIOUS MU a vATrI3II TEA. SOLE AGENTS MINDY MILLING AND Co., LTD. WIIOLESALS AND RETAIL GROCERS ETC., 5, RORSEMARICET-STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>HOW TROOPER SWANN MET HisIEARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON. DEATH. – P.S.A.</head>
        <p>HOW TROOPER SWANN MET HisIEARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON. DEATH. - P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the Earlestown and District P.S.A. Society, was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall, on Sunday afternoon. Mr G Dring pre- sided, and an excellent address was given by Mr W H Dale. Vocal selections were given by the P.S.A. choir, and a recitation "Trouble in the Amen Corner," was given by Mr Platt TENNIS CLUB BALL AT NEWTON. . _</p>
        <p>The annual ball in connection with the Newton- le-Willows Tennis Club took place on Friday even- ing in the Town Hall, Newton-le-Willows. The hall was neatly decorated for the occasion, and about 40 couples were present. Mr. Pearson's (Wigan) band supplied the music, and dancing was kept up with much vigour until five o'clock next morning. WARGRAVE CHURCH. The annual sermons in connection with the Foreign Missions were preached in Wargrave Church on Sunday morning and evening by the Rev WII Barnes, at one time Missionary in the Sandwich Islands. In the afternoon the rev gentle- man gave an address to the scholars, and collec- tions were made in aid of the funds of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel. THEFT FROM A PUBLIC-HOUSE. At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, before Messrs S R At mitage and J Ball, Thomas Langan was charged with stealing a spirit glass value 41,, the property of William Beatty. at Earlestown on the 10th inst.—Margaret, Ann Ken- wright, a girl residing at 84, Leigh-street, stated that she was employed at the " Griffin Hotel." She was at the bar at six o'clock on the evening in question and saw the prisoner take the spirit glass off the counter and leave the house—William Beatty the licensee of the Hotel, said that in consequence of what he heard he went in search of the prisoner and found him in company with another man. He accused both of stealing the glass and prisoner took it out of his pocket and gave it to him—Prisoner who stated that he was drunk when he took the glass, was fined 10s, NEWTON-LE...WILLOWS ‘IIITUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of -t-l;"; -1-l'oove, a literary enter- tainment was given in the Congregational school- room on Monday evening. There was a very fair attendance, and the entertainment consisted of reci- tals from popular authors by Mr W S Valiance. The Rev James Allatt presided, and in introducing Mr Valiance, briefly referred in eulogistic terms to his abilities as an 'elocutionist. The following pro- gramme was submitted, and Proved most interest- ing :—" A Story of the Rail " (Sims), " Godfrey Grubb" (Macrae), "That blue-eyed Colleen " (Mcßwan), " The burial of General Wauchope " (tlales), " Owen More " (Overton), " Death of Mr Maclure " (lan Maclaren), " Cooley, his Boy and his Dog " (Max Adeler), "The Diamond Wedding (Anon). " The McWilliams and the Lightning (Mark Twain), and " The lesson of the Watermill." EARLESTOWN GOOD TEMPLAILEI</p>
        <p>The usual weakly session was held in th- Co-operative Ball, Wargrave-road, on Monday even ing, when a good attendance was presided over by Bro T Cutler, CT. After the usual opening, one candidate was admitted to membership. and at 8.30 all further business was postponed and the meeting opened to non-members, a good number availing themselves of this privilege Bro W Roberts, F. Sec., who was responsible for the programme, pre- sided, and afte•• a few preliminaries the foll owing items were submitted and were without exception capitally rendered and well received :—Solo piano, "The Ash Grove," Mr A Woodcock: song, " When the heart is young," Miss L Wright ; recitation, "The Diamond Brooch." Mr J Roberts ; mouth organ, " Selections," Mr J Ellison ; song, " Angus Macdonald," Miss A Goslin; recitation, " The I Signalman's Story," Mr D Adcock; recitations, "The Indian Mutiny" and "Wreck of the Atlantic," Mr W Harrison ; solo piano, `. Return from Klondvice," Mr A Woodcock : recitation," The Lancashire's at Spion Roy," Mr C Drew.—Fleartv thanks were accorded all friends assisting in making these meet- ings so enjoyable, on the motion of Bro T Woods LD, seconded by Bro J Warburton. A very plese- ant evening was concluded at 9.40.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Graphic Accounts of the Fight at Ham elfontein.</head>
        <p>Graphic Accounts of the Fight at Ham elfontein.</p>
        <p>The followino descriptive acclunt of the fight at Flamelfontein on D,cember 17th in which the late Trooper Swann, met his death hal; been received from a trooper of the 32nd Company I. Y., whose parents I live at Brynn.</p>
        <p>Colesberg. December 19th, 1900. Just a few lines to let you know that I am still in the land of the living, and in the very best of health, strength and spirits under the circumstances. As no doubt you will have seen by the papers before your receive this letter we have been in a very warm corner, and I am very sorry to say that we have lost two brave men killed, seven wounded and seven more taken prisoners. We had only 28 men to start with, so you may know that we were hard pressed, as we had only 12 men left to fight the battle out against 250 Been who had com- pletely surrounded us. We were fighting from half-past ten in the morning until after eight at night, the only rest we had was while the Boers came in twice under the white flag asking us to surrender, as it was useless for us to try and hold out. So our Lieutenant called us together and asked us which we would rather do, fight or give in. We sent word back that we would fight to the last man. I was one of five who were told off to hold a kook, below the piequet kopje, which the Boere cap- tured, killing Sam Swann the first minute, so they took the other four men who were with him prisoners. The Boers were in possession of the kopje above ue,and they made it very hot for U 9, as they could fire down on 09, although we managed to hold it for a couple of hours. Then we retired to the water sluit. which four of ue held for six hours, but there was only one left out of the four when the six hours were up, as one was killed and and the other two very badly wounded, and how I pulled through God above only knows, as the bullets were thicker than hailstones, and when you get about 10 hours among it you have had nearly enough for once. What few were left retired to the house. In so doing sor., had to cross a level piece of ground about 50 yards. Every man who crossed had about four volleys put into him. I and another got in safely, but the next two were dropped before they landed in. They all thought I was killed, as one of those explosive bullets went right through my jacket pocket into the earth on which I was lying and exploded, throwing me right into the trench. I was rather stunned for a few minutes but soon came alright again. What few were able to get into the house fixed bayonets, and guarded the windows and doors. The Boers kept firing for two hours after dark, while thEy buried their dead. They took what wounded they could with them, but two of their men who were nearly dead were ' sent into us, as they could do no good with them At daybreak this morning the Lieutenant asked me if I would volunteer to ride a despatch through to Coleeberg for reinforcements and doctors, which I did, taking two of the best horses that we had, so that if one got shot I could mount the other. but lam glad to say I got through alright and a column with two guns has started out to reinforce at Harnelfontein. The Commandant at this place would not let me go with them, he said I had done enough for once and both my horses were done ute. so I bad orders to stop here until this morning. I am expecting orders every minute to start. Sam Swann, who was killed, is the big fellow on that photo that I sent with my tent's company on, and I can tell you that I am awfully sorry about him, as we have been together ever since we came over. He was one of the beet soldiers that ever crossed the water. The other fellow who was killed is named Johnson from Liverpool Wooltoni. He was a Lieutenant over there in the Volunteer Artillery, but became out here as a private to us, and a very nice fellow he was. He dropped on the top of me when he fell, the bullet passing right through him. Joe Hill is alright, but Boardman is taken prisoner, so I don't iipow how he has fared. lam afraid we are going to have a very rough Christmas, as 1,600 Boers have crossed the river close to Harnelfontein. The following sympathetic letter, bearing on the same event has been received'bv Mrs Swann, from Lieutenant W. A. L. Fletcher of the 3End Co., I. Y. Hamelfontetn. Near Coleeberg. 20/12/00. The sad news of the death ef your son will have already' reached'you. lam writing to offer to you my most sincere sympathy in your trouble. Your son was in charge of a piequet on a kopje a little distance from camp. He saws Boers approaching and epened fin on them at once. The Beers got on to a neighbouring kopje from which they opened a heavy fire, which rendered it impossible for the picquet to show themselves without being hit. He tried to prevent the kopje being taken by returning the fire,but that meant exposing himself, and was promptly shot down, He was a fine and brave soldier, arid would, I know, far sooner have died, as he did, than to have lived a few minutes longer to have seen his poet captured. He lies buried in view of the place which he gave his life to hold. A. temporary cross is at the head, and his grave is fenced round. I will try to get a Photograph taken of the grave, which I will send you' IDEICEI more, allow me to express my sincere sympathy, and I remain, Yours truly, W A L Fletcher. To Mrs Swann. Corporal Arthur 0 wbridge of EarleStown, writes from Hamelfontein.</p>
        <p>"It is with blter grief I write these few lines, you will understand the reason se hy. I have lest my com- rade Dear old Sam' he has been a dear comrade to me, one that you reed about, but seldom find in this stern world of ours. It may be some consolation to his parents to know that he died fighting for his ceuetry that he loved so well and that he died like a true British soldier, face to the foe. I will try to give you some little idea of how it was done. I had been on guard all night. There was only me and another Corporal here, so Sam being the oldest soldier,he was sent out in charge of my relief. Before leaving the kopje I had a few words with him, a joke or two, and he borrowed my field glasses as he had lest his two months ago. I left him and went down to my breakfast about 9.15, then went to the brook, was having a wash, and I heard some shots fired and one of our chaos said the Boers were on us. I started to run for the farm, but on a second thought returned to fill my water bottle, and a good thing I did. It WAS all the water there was for four of us until nine o'clock at night It appears that dear o'd Sam WILS laying his thing' down, when he saw the 830f3 making for a kleje that commanded ours, and gave the word to fire on them. He said " wiz, away lads." No Conner had he got the words out than a bullet struck him in the right side, passing through his body. He lived for half an hour.the remainder of the picket serrendering a little lv,er. The Boers went through his pockets and took everything he had, and let the remain ler of the picket go to him. He was conscious a short time, but did not 30• M te realise his time was so short. He !lad said " I caree cre,rile breath" and started to try and sing, and the dear old boy was trying to sing when he died, game to the last. If he had known he was to have died, it was the death he would have chosen, for he often said tome that he never wished tolay long, and poor old bey, be had his wish. The engagement lasted from 9.30 a.m. tO9 o'clock at night. Our small farce consisted of 20 Lancers, the officer,whose conduct was simply grand,eight Grenadier Guards, and two of Nesbits horse. Two of our chaps were ' killed, seven taken prisoners,and five wounded, and two 9 13 rifles left to fight attacked by 250 Boers. wGuitahr.ds vywhoeunedletcle,ileox whaevwe eornel who were reinforced by 50 more at midday, you will wonelnr how we did it. I simply can't say, for not till then did I know what an Englishman was made of. Our only fear was that we should have to surrender, and Yet we were determined we would not. The enemy's bullets came like hail stones, and my word, svhsts the Boer finds his range, he (ran shoot. They s •nt to ask us to surrender about 10 30 by the flag of truce, but our officer, Mr Fletcher. simply said " no," nor, as lonelae his men would hold out. He told us, and it was just" Hear, hear," and we got ready. Time after time we thought it was all over with yards of us, and all of us had us, as they got adsomethverynoarwroitic within Oghlasves. Some held the farm house, and some in a short trench juqt outside, and I, along with two or three others held the stables. We fired through the windows and doors,'and punched holes through the walls. The stable only had a zinc roof, and it was simply riddled, and two of our horses were shot inside and 5 out. I think by this time you will be able to judge what we have done. Our Captain came in yesterday, the 19th. Be congratulated me and others and told us we had saved Colesberg, but at what a cost, me old comrade gone it takes all the shine out of it. They have sent in a splendid report. I don't know whether you will see it, as we have no newspaper men hero. Ou^ captain came in with the other two troops, the Coldstreama, 5 Lancers. 2 guns of the 44th battery and 30 Nesbite horse. When firing ceased at night, they asked us to take two of their wounded in who were ton b'd to be removed, u ", a 3,0%. US to treat them kindly, and we could not do anything else, as thee were very good to Our &amp;laps that they caught." Referring again to Troopor Swam-in, he R‘id he did not think 'here was a sinele chao in the whole world who wiqhfel him any harm. "Everywhere he went he was so popular. Even the Grmadiers that were vith us, say they hive lose a mat'. If, wee only a night or two ago since ho was singing with them. He is buried within at few yards from where I am writing, and some of the Coldetreame are doing his grave up, and putting a wire fence round."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>DELICIOUS MAZAWATTER TRA DELICIOUS MAZAWATTEE TEA DELICIOUS AIAZAMATTHE TEA SOLE AGENTS : HENRY MILLING AND CO • LTD. • WHOLESALE AND RETAIL GROCERS ETC., 5 EIJR3ENILRIU'r-MF:r, W ARRINGTaX.</head>
        <p>DELICIOUS MAZAWATTER TRA DELICIOUS MAZAWATTEE TEA DELICIOUS AIAZAMATTHE TEA SOLE AGENTS : HENRY MILLING AND CO • LTD. • WHOLESALE AND RETAIL GROCERS ETC., 5 EIJR3ENILRIU'r-MF:r, W ARRINGTaX.</p>
