      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH SUNDAY</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH SUNDAY</p>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Prize Distribution at Earlestown.</p>
        <p>On Moolay evening, the Mission Hall, Earles,own, as prowl el to overo ervi eg no f'he oclasion of the nnual dis•ribution 'IF prizes to tho schola rs of St. John's Church Sunday School. The Rev. T. R. Matthews pre- sided, and during an interval in the entertainmen,, and prior to the prizes being handed out, said it WS with much pleasure that he submitted to them the report of the General Secretary ha which on behalf of the Superintendents and teachers he tendered thanks for the very liberal response to their appeal for financial aid, and although their expenses had been heavy, they had a small balance in hand, rhn school was divii-d into three seetions,which were again divided into 48 classes. They had eleven efficers, 45 teachers, and 1,089 scholars. The scholars were distributed as follows :—Men's Bible Class. 134, conducted by Mr. Molvneux ; young ladies' bible class 65.Iconducted by Mrs. Wilson ; girls' school 346, Super- intendent Mr. Burrows: boys' school 226. Superinten- dent Mr. Clayton ; infants 221. In addition to these there was the Mission School. The wizes for attendance and lessons won this year numbered 199 as against 147 last year. The marks averaged 101 per prize winnor—eirls 102, and boys 100—The income was £5l 124 211 and expenditure £5l. lie 61d, leaving a balance in the hands of the treasurer of eightpence. That, he said, was a satisfactory report. He, however, was very greedy. He would rather have a better report, more scholars, nio-e teachers, more officers, more prizes. Of course that would mean more money but they would get it somehow. Out of nearly 1,100 scholars, only 198 had earned prizes. Why had not the. other 900 earned prizes,because they had not attended regularly and had not done their work well. In addition to the prizes they saw there. they distributed to the very small children, nearly 200 cards. He limped next year to see a greater improvement in the attend- ance, in the conduct and in the lesions. There was room for all to imnrove and mend. During the past year two of their scholars had been lately taken away to a better land, and both of the scholars would have had prizes had they lived, but he had advised the secretary that prizes should be provided and handed over to the sorrowing parents of the childreh. The rev gentleman then proceeded to distribute the awards. . . .</p>
        <p>- The entertianment was provided by the infant scholars of the District School. under the supervision of Mrs. Brown,being a repetition of a portion of the programme submitted a short time back by the same children at their annual concert, and included character songs, recitations, solos, dialogues and dances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>PEN KETH.</head>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>BAND OF floe&amp; —On Friday last the annual tea meet- ing in connection with the Penbeth Wesleyan Band of Hope cry' held in the Wesleyan schools. The tables were tastefully decorated, and the room was well filled with members and friends. Tea was set for the junior mem- beta at rive o'clock. and was again prepared for the adult members at six o'clock. After tea the Rev John Gunnell addressed the audience on the principles of temperance. During the evening various kinds of indoor games were induked in. PARISH COUNCIL</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the above Council was held at the residence of Mr It Garnett, Hall Nook, Penk.th, the following men:there being present—Dr Murray (chairman), Mr R Garnett (vice-chairman), Mews J S Snodrly, E W Clarke, .7 Millington, C E Parker, and E Foster (clerk).</p>
        <p>Correspondence was read from the Rural District Council, stating that instructions had been given to the Corporation to erect a gas lame in Station-road opposite to Mersey View:—lt was stated that the gas lamp in question had been erected, and was lighted for the first time on Tuesday evening and was high'y appreciated by the residents.—The foot"paths in the neir,bourhood that had been described at the last meeting as being "in a die- graceful state of repair " had now been attended to.— This much discussed question was again brought to the front, and correspondence was read from the Great Sankey Parish Council in reply to a letter sent frarn this Council asking them to erect a lamp on the Sankey side of Halten's Bridge. However, they replied that they (the Sankey Parish Council) had no objections to the Penketh Council erecting a lamp or lamps on the high- way as indicated. This reply did not appear to meet the case as intended by this Council, and the following resolution was proposed by Mr Snoddy and carried unanimously, viz :—" We feel very strangly the dangerous condition of the bend in the road on tho San- key side, and are very anxious to do all in our power to lessen the danger, as we feel the responsibility as a Coun- cil in this matter. We have taken the advice of the Surveyor with respect to the lighting, and his opinion is that a lamp on the Penketle side of the bridge would be useless unlees your Council will also put one on the Sankey side of the bend, We trust that your Council will seriously consider the danger we wish to safeguard against, and cl-operate with us in minimizing it as far as lies in our power."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
