      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."]</p>
        <p>Warrington expected two points last Saturday and they got them, but not before they had had a severe fright and gone as near losing as was possible. Wigan's recent improved play was taken no account of, and drawing a line through past performances and particu- larly the one at Wigan early in the season, the War- rington players concluded that they had, in football parlance, "a sweet thing on." How sweet a thing it was, may be imagined when the fact is recalled that at half-time the local team was seven points in arrear. This was mostly due to their own lack of energy and over confidence, but in a great measure the determined play of the Wiganers was the source of the difficulty the home players found themselves in.</p>
        <p>Warrington ought to have tumbled to the fact much sooner that Wigan were going to prove a hard nut to crack, especially as the home team was short of half the best players. Instead, they ambled round like kittens with their eyes shut, and the visitors were all the time straining every nerve to get a lead. There was nothing particularly smart about the efforts on tho Wigan side but there was no end of determination and energy. A penalty goal from Wigan's new threequarter was the first item of note, and before Warrington had quite got over the shock a goal and a try were added in excellent style. The seven points deficit acted like scold douche on the laggard Warringtonians and they began to make things hum to's livelier tune. Wigan would not be shaken off, however and kept their handsome lead till the interval.</p>
        <p>Both sides took the field again with a full determina- tion to win, and the home lot smarting under a curtain lecture in addition. Progress was not reported for some time, as the lusty coal-heavers adopted defensive tactics which were difficult to defeat. Near three-quarter time the home players and spectators alike got desperate. The latter shouted frantically, fiercely, and encourag- ingly, and the formr showed a dash which boded ill for the seven points deficit. Harris very smartly took a half-opportunity which came his way and opened the scoring with a try at the corner worthy of his best days. Mighty efforts followed and the leaders were gradually but surely overplayed. It was near the end, though. before Dickenson outwitted the Wigan backs and scored in so good a position that Ratciiffe had no difficulty in kicking the winning goal.</p>
        <p>Loud and long were the cheers which pro. claimed Warrington's victory, but the general feeling was one of sympathy with the losers. They de- served to win, undoubtedly, and Warrington's snatch- point victory was a piece of hard luck for the persever- ing Wiganers. I will not go so far as to say they were the t.etter team, but there was certainly very little to choose between the teams on the day's play. Warrington shaped so miserably both forward and back during a great portion of the game that they appeared to be quite outclassed. Wigan are very deficient in cleverness and the finer points of the game, and make up for much of this by great strength forward, where they had a great pull. Most of the time a couple of Wigan ecrimmagers were " winging it" and devoting their attentions to the Warrin ;ton halves, yet the remaining six had no difficulty in holding up the home eight. In Wigan strong forwards appear to be a natural product of the soil. In Warrington no amount of forcing and artificial rearing will bring them forth.</p>
        <p>I don't know that Warrington, though the winners were much superior to the losers in any department. Hallam was certainly the best of the two full backs, though he was not so smart as usual, and perhaps R Bate did the most valuable work at half-back. Burgess was a terrible failure and never gave a decent pass during the whole of the game. The two right wing men, both named Harris, played well for their respective sides, but not much can be said for the rest of the backs.</p>
        <p>When Warrington have Fish, Isherwood. Duckworth, Morrison, Mereweather, Lawless and Scott available once more we shall imagine we can command a really strong team, but the results of matches will be little better as past history shows. The W wrington men are nearly all on the same level of ability and that level is not high enough. Class is still the one thing needful.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows For Apt. P. W. L. D. G T. P. a T. P. Pt Swinton 17...17... 0... 0...22 57-215-11 5 37...34 Oldham 17...14... 3... 0...20 57-211...12 10— 54...28 Runcorn 18...14... 4... 0...34 74-176...11 18— 76-28 Broughton R'ng'519...11... 6... 2-27 37-165...14 16— 76...24 Salford 18...10... 8.- 0...25 36-158...22 22-110...20 Leigh 18... 9... 9... 0...20 24-112.-16 27-113...18 Warrington 18... 8... 9... 1...15 24-102...16 23-101_17 Barrow 17... 8... 9... 0...19 27-119...19 26-116...16 Rochdale Hornetsl7... 5-10.- 2...15 1.2 66...27 44-186...12 Stockport 18. 4...11... 3...10 14— 61..19 24-11 -.11 filillom l6. 5...11... 0...15 12— 66...20 25-115...10 .St. Helens 18.. 4...12... 2...11 12— 58...27 38...168... 8 Widnes 16... 4...12... 0...13 10— 56...18 48-189—'8 Wigan _ 17... 3...12... 2...12 12— 60...26 42-178... 8 .</p>
        <p>Helens have had two points deducted for a breach of the rules.</p>
        <p>Warrington have still mov, losses than wins standing to their name, while the point average has deteriorated woefully in the last two months. At present the record is 102 points for and 101 points against. Are we to see the race for supremacy won by the latter figure or the former ? In other werds is the Warrington team going to do better or worse? Probably neither.</p>
        <p>The " grate fite "at Oldham on Saturday, in which the home club and Swinton were engaged turned out to be a hard game, fought under excitable conditions, which did not tend to make the proceedings like unto the manners of folks at a drawing-room entertainment. Swinton were favoured by Fortune in being able to score when they did, and Oldham are entitled to sym- pathy for their ill luck. By seven points to five Swin- ton secured their seventeenth successive victory in the Competition, and are now leading with plenty of mar- gin. Protests have been made by both sides owing to the encroachment of the huge crowd in the playing portion of the field, but Swinton have, naturally, not pressed their's.</p>
        <p>Other matches resulted pretty much as expected, though Barrow were hardly thought good enough to de- feat the Hornetsby over 20 poin,s. This they have done, however. Bravo, Barrow !</p>
        <p>The fixtures for this Saturday are as follows : Millom v Warrington. L?igh v Runcorn. Barrow v Widnes. St Helens v Swinton. St Herd v Broughton Rangers. Stockport v Oldham. Wigan v Rochdale Hornets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>Gymnastics.</head>
        <p>Gymnastics.</p>
        <p>Some intere;ting competitions are to take place in connection with the Liverpool and District Shield Com- petition. The draw in the first round will be as fol- lows :—Liverpool : Gordon Institute v Birkenhead Y.M.C.I. Gymnasium, St Philip's Gymnasium v Korth Liverpool Gymnasium, Colosseum v Balfour Institute, Warrington Gymnasium a bye. In the boys' competi- tion, the following teams are drawn together in the first round :—Guild of St Martin v Colosseum Juniors, Warrington v St Helens Y.M.C.A., North Liverpool v " Colly Kids." In order to encourage girls to take a further interest in physical training, the National Physical Recreation Society have granted a 25-guinea shield for competition amongst the girls' classes of Liverpool and district. This season's competition will take place during March, the item being dumb-bell exercise.</p>
        <p>Angling. The new association has commenced, an from the number of members it enrolled the first night (Monday last) the prospect should be encouraging. it must have been very gratifying to the promoters when only a very few short of one hundred joined on the first night. The club has decided besides the prizes offerel a week or so ago to give set prizes to the value of 101 shillings each for gudgeon, trout, dace, rolch, perch, bream, chub, daddy rough, and a special prize of 53 for the largest pike Caught during the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>TEMPERANCE MEETING.—In connection with the Free o Church Council a public meeting was held ha the Town Hall on Saturday evening, presided ver by Mr Thomas Earlam. The Rev A H Eynaston gave an interesting address. YOUNG MEN'S GUILD —On Saturday evening the first meeting of the Guild formed for the senior classes of the Trinity Sunday School was held. Books and papers, with numerous games, have been arranged for the meetings. The offic3re are as follows :—President, Rev T H Bailey ; treasurer, Mr F Boston ; secretary, Mr Harry Cawley.</p>
