      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-01-18_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington and District League. Second Division.</p>
        <p>BRITANNIA COMBINATION V. ST. AUSTIN'S.— At Latchford. The locals pressed from the start, but the visitors put on a splendid defence to keep their goal clear for some time. After some even plav,Bailey broke away. and passed to Sherrat,who scored. Half-time : Britannia, one goal; St. Austin's, nil. On turning round, the hoinesters kept up the attack. Newell, after a fine run was robbed by Beck. Britannia now showed grand form, and after some erratic shooting, F Knight added a second. goal Final:—Britannia, two gel's; St. Austin, nit WARRINGTON ATHLETIC V. TRINITY—FIayed on the ground of the latter on Saturday. The visitors opened I the attack,Kenyon and Fielding having hard lines. Baker relieved with a good run, but Fielding got play back again. A center in favour of the Athletic was headed out by Shearer. A good passing movement by Baker, Hewitt, and Maar. ended in Baker beating Hamlett, but the point was die-allowed for off-die. Parry was called upon to save a shot from the foot of Pennington. A free kick. against the locals was cleared by Jones. Half-time : Warrington Athletic, nil ; Warrington Trinity, nil. The visitors still kept up the pressure, Parry being called up on to clear on several occasions. Jones gave Trinity the lead from a penalty. Final :—Warrineton Trinity, one go Warrington Athletic, nil. Elia;riestown Rovers v. Thatto Heath, The Rovers dropped two valuable points oa Saturday when they allowed Thatto Heath to defeat them by two goals to one. The Rovers took matters far too easily and they suffered for it. Their chance of the championship of the Liverpool and District Amateur League is rapidly fading away, as their victors of Saturday now step above them. Newton-le-Willows v. White Star Wanderers (The Combination). Played at Newton-le-Willows on Saturday. The homasters[started, and play for the fint few minutes wa9 confined to the home halt. After Borrock had saved. Cunningham secured and opened play with a splendid shot, which brought Horrocks to his knees in a feeble effort to save. White star initiated another prolonged attacked on the home goal. The defence, however, responded well, and Newton, dashing down, Houghton scored a grand goal, Hodge misjudging the shot. Straightway the visitors advanced on the Newton goal. and Beckett scored with a shot which passed through a a host of players. The interval was announced with the Star possessing a deserved lead of two goals to one. Ors resuming Newton were the first to take up the running, Houghton dribbling well dwn and theta shooting wide. The homesters again returned to the attack, but the efforts of the forwardS in front of goal were unaccountably weak and erratic, Hedge having very few difficult shots to deal with. A combined move by the Star forwards was the means of transferring play but the Newton backs easily kept them at bay although a commendable effort from Beckett narrowly missed scoring. After about twenty minutee play a bully in the Star goal resulted in Lee defeating Hodge from close quarters, the custodian having no chance of saving. Both teams tried hard to gain the lead after this but the final eventually came with the score of two goals each unaltered. As this is the first point Newton have picked up since October, perhaps their luck is going to turn. However, they seem to have a very fair team and have this week signed on a new centure-forward named Mayor a local youth who has been playing with one of the junior clubs in the dis- trict.</p>
      </div>
