        <p>3. Do you suffer from fainting fits ? Is your vision defective? Does your heirt palpitate on the least exertion? Are you pale and thin ? Is there a shed. ness of breath with cold hands and feet ? 4. Is there a tightness in the chest with shooting pains increased by breathing or exertion ? IS there a sense of choking or fulness in the throat as if something was rising to the throat? Do yott feel feeble, listless, lacking ambition and energy? If so. your heart is weak, circulation poor, vitality low,whicla will frequently be accompanied by weak stomach and constipation. You require more blood, a stronger heart, better circulation and greater vitality. For all these symutorns there is no medicine in England so effective as Veno's Seaweed Tonic. It is "The people's strong. thener and health giver." A scientific remedy possess. ing the greatest therapeutic value. It is made to act specifically upon the stomach, liver, kidneys, blood and heart. A book relating to diseases and how to cure ' them accompanies each bottle. Ask for Veno's Seaweed Tonic, but be ALM you get it. Price Is lid and 219 d. VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE stops a cough in one night, and rapidly cures the worst and most desperate cases of bronchitis, asthrna,inflaenze and whooping cough. It has saved many lives after they had been turned out of hospitals. It is a new scientific) remedy composed of ingiedients never before used in England. Ask for Veno's Lightning Cough Cure. Price Isld and 23 9d. Sold by Boots Ltd., Warrington; Peake, Earlestown and Newton; Weston and Co., Run-, corn ; Wallbridge, St Helens and all chemists.</p>
        <p>Personal. During the hearing of a case of cruelty to a mare at the Borough Police Court on Monday the Chief Constable said he wished he could have had the evidence of Inspector Brown. He was afraid that the Inspector, who had been ill, would never be seen in the court again, as he had severed his con- nection with the society, mainly through ill health. Mr John Crosfield, J.P., we are pleased to learn, is benefiting greatly by his stay at Folkestone.</p>
